var WK_INDONESIA = [
{"id":"indonesia-rendang","name":"Rendang","nameAlt":"Rendang — not a curry at all, but beef that slowly stops simmering and starts frying in the oil its own coconut milk gives up","aliases":["Rendang","Beef Rendang","Rendang Daging","Rendang Padang","Randang","Kalio","Indonesian Dry Beef Curry"],"course":"main","type":["main","slow-cooked","curry","beef","spiced","make-ahead","festive"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","sunday","celebration","make-ahead","guests"],"ingredients":"180g beef chuck · 160ml coconut milk · 60g shallots · 20g red chilli · 15g garlic · 12g galangal · 12g lemongrass · 10g fresh ginger · 15g desiccated coconut · 8g macadamia nuts · 5g ground turmeric · 5g tamarind paste · 4g salt · 3g cinnamon","method":"Start with the cut, because rendang is one of the few dishes where the cheap piece is not a compromise but the only correct choice. Use the 180g of beef chuck and do not trim it clean. Chuck is shoulder — it is threaded with collagen and seams of fat, and over the next three hours that collagen dissolves into gelatine and gives the meat the soft, slightly sticky give that rendang is famous for. A lean piece like rump or fillet has nothing to dissolve; it simply dries into fibres and no amount of time will save it. Cut the chuck across the grain into chunks about 3cm square. Across the grain matters: it shortens the muscle fibres so each piece falls apart in the mouth rather than shredding into strings.\n\nNow the bumbu, which is the paste that does all the work. Peel and roughly chop the 60g of shallots, the 15g of garlic, the 12g of galangal and the 10g of fresh ginger. Trim the 12g of lemongrass down to the pale bottom third and slice it thinly across — lemongrass is woody and a blender will not break it down, so pre-slicing it thinly is what stops you finding splinters in the finished dish. Add the 20g of red chilli, the 8g of macadamia nuts, the 5g of ground turmeric and the 3g of cinnamon. Blitz all of it to a genuine paste in a small processor, and here is the important part: LOOSEN IT WITH A LITTLE OF THE COCONUT MILK, NOT WITH WATER. Take about 30ml off the 160ml and use that. Water dilutes the dish and then has to be boiled away again, which wastes an hour; coconut milk is going into the pot anyway. Blitz until there is no grit left between your fingers when you rub a little between them. Grit in the paste is grit in the finished rendang, and it never disappears with cooking.\n\nThe macadamias are standing in for candlenuts, and they are doing a real job rather than a decorative one. Candlenut is used across Indonesia purely as a thickener and an emulsifier — it is faintly bitter and mildly toxic raw, and nobody eats it for flavour. What it provides is fat and starch that hold the paste together and give the sauce body. Macadamia is the standard substitute worldwide because the fat content is close, and in South Africa it happens to be the local nut rather than the imported one.\n\nMake the kerisik next and do not skip it, because it is the difference between a good coconut stew and rendang. Put the 15g of desiccated coconut in a dry frying pan, no oil, over medium-low heat, and stir it more or less continuously for 5 to 8 minutes. It will go from white to pale cream to biscuit to a deep nutty brown, and it moves fast at the end — the last thirty seconds are where it burns, so pull it off the heat while it is still the colour of dark toast and not the colour of coffee. Tip it straight out of the pan onto a cold plate; a hot pan carries on cooking it. Then grind it, in a mortar or a spice grinder, until the oil comes out and it clumps into a coarse, damp, fragrant paste rather than a powder. That toasted coconut paste is a thickener, a sweetener and a smoky bottom note all at once, and it is why real rendang tastes of something that has been near a fire.\n\nNow the step that goes against everything else you know about braising: DO NOT BROWN THE MEAT. There is no searing in rendang, and no frying of the paste first either. Put the raw beef, the bumbu, the remaining 130ml of coconut milk and the 5g of tamarind paste into a wide heavy pan — wide, not deep, because you want surface area for evaporation — and stir it into one pale, slack, uniform mass while it is still cold. Then bring it up to a bare simmer. The browning in this dish happens at the very end, in the fat that separates out, and it happens to the meat and the paste together. Sear it first and you get two separate flavours instead of one.\n\nSimmer it uncovered, on the lowest heat that keeps a lazy bubble breaking the surface, for about two hours. Stir every fifteen or twenty minutes, and scrape the bottom of the pan properly each time — coconut solids and the paste both settle and catch, and a single scorched patch flavours the entire pot bitter. Do not cover it. A lid returns every drop of steam to the pan and you would be simmering forever. Over these two hours the liquid drops from a soupy white to a thick golden gravy and the beef goes from firm to just yielding.\n\nAround the two-hour mark you have a dish with its own name. When the sauce is a rich golden-brown gravy that still coats a spoon and pours, that is KALIO — the wet, half-way rendang eaten all over West Sumatra in its own right, and if you stop here, salt it and serve it, nobody has made a mistake. But rendang proper is further on.\n\nStir in the kerisik and the 4g of salt now, and start paying attention, because the next thirty minutes contain the moment the dish changes character. Keep the heat low and stir more often, then almost constantly. The gravy thickens, then goes glossy, then starts to look slightly broken and grainy — and then a clear, faintly golden oil comes out of it and pools around the meat. THIS IS PECAH MINYAK, THE BREAK, and it is the whole point. Coconut milk is an emulsion of coconut oil held in water by protein. As the last of the water leaves, the emulsion cannot hold and the oil comes out on its own. Nothing is wrong. What has happened is that your pot has quietly stopped being a simmer and become a shallow fry, and the beef is now frying in pure coconut oil coated in its own spice paste.\n\nFrom the break, give it another 20 to 30 minutes and DO NOT STOP STIRRING for more than a few seconds at a time, because there is no water left to protect anything from the base of the pan. The colour deepens from red-brown to dark mahogany, almost black at the edges, and the paste stops being a sauce and becomes a dry crust clinging to each piece. This is where the flavour you cannot get any other way is made — the sugars in the shallots and the coconut are browning in hot oil, not stewing in water. It is done when you can drag a spoon across the base and the track stays open for a moment before the oil creeps back, and when the meat looks almost dry but glistens.\n\nTaste it and adjust the salt at the very end, never earlier. This dish reduces to somewhere near a fifth of its starting volume, so a pot salted correctly at the beginning is inedible by the end. Let it stand at least twenty minutes off the heat before eating. And if you can leave it until tomorrow, do — the spice paste keeps moving into the meat overnight, and rendang made today and eaten tomorrow is a noticeably better dish than rendang eaten hot from the pan.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Simmer 2 hr · Fry down 30 min","kcal":"~610 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 39g, Carbs 14g, Fat 44g, Sodium ~880mg","storage":"Rendang is the rare dish that is better on day two and day three than it is on day one, and it is worth knowing why: the paste is still migrating into the meat, and the fat sets and re-melts carrying flavour with it. Cool it uncovered to room temperature, then fridge it in a closed container for up to 5 days. It will set solid with a cap of pale coconut fat on top — stir that fat back in when you reheat, it is the dish, not waste. Reheat gently in a pan with a tablespoon of water to loosen it, never in a hot pan on its own, or the crust catches. It freezes better than almost anything else in this book, 3 months with no loss at all, because there is very little water left in it to form ice crystals. Two honest warnings about the folklore. Properly dry rendang genuinely does keep for weeks unrefrigerated in West Sumatra — the water is gone, the fat seals the surface and the spices are antimicrobial — but that applies to rendang taken all the way to black and dry, in a scrupulously clean pot, and a Pretoria kitchen in February is not the place to test the theory. Fridge it. And do not try to shortcut the standing time by keeping it warm on a low plate for hours; it dries past pleasant into leathery.","chefNotes":"Chuck, not a lean cut, cut across the grain. Loosen the bumbu with coconut milk instead of water. No searing — everything goes into a cold wide pan together. Then wait for pecah minyak, the moment the oil separates out and the simmer becomes a fry, and give it another half hour of near-constant stirring after that. Salt only at the end, because the pot reduces to a fifth.","pairsWith":"Plain steamed white rice and nothing clever — rendang is intensely concentrated and the rice is there to carry it, not to compete. What it really wants alongside is something raw, sharp and cooling: sliced cucumber, a wedge of lime, and a spoonful of sambal on the side of the plate for heat that is bright rather than deep. A plate of fried tempeh gives it crunch, and a simple boiled green vegetable stops the whole meal being rich in one register.","trivia":"Rendang is not the name of a dish. It is the name of a technique — from the Minangkabau word merandang, meaning to slowly reduce something until the liquid is gone — which is why the same method applied to chicken, jackfruit, egg or tempeh is still rendang, and why the wet halfway stage has its own separate name, kalio, rather than being called unfinished rendang. What looks like an elaborate curry is really a preservation method wearing a curry's clothes. Take the water out of a food and there is nothing left for bacteria to live in; finish it in coconut oil and you have sealed it; do it with galangal, turmeric, lemongrass and chilli and you have added a genuinely antimicrobial spice load on top. The result keeps for weeks at tropical room temperature without a fridge.\n\nThat is precisely what it was for. Minangkabau culture in West Sumatra has a long tradition called merantau — young men leaving the home village to make their way in the wider world — and rendang was the food that travelled with them, in a tin, for a journey measured in days or weeks. It is also why Padang restaurants, the Minangkabau eating houses now found in every Indonesian city, stack their rendang in the front window at room temperature all day and nobody worries. The same logic explains rendang's ceremonial weight: it is the dish cooked for weddings and for the end of Ramadan, brought as a gift to a house, because it is one of the very few celebration foods that can be made days in advance and improves by waiting. The Minangkabau are also matrilineal, with houses and land passing down the female line, and the deep knowledge of when a pot has broken and when it is finished has traditionally passed the same way — by watching, in a kitchen, not by any written time.","howThisFeels":"The moment the sauce quietly gives up and a slick of clear oil rises around the meat, and the smell in the kitchen turns from coconut to toasted spice in about ninety seconds.","leftovers":["(easy) Rendang on hot white rice with a fried egg on top and a squeeze of lime is the standard next-day lunch across Indonesia, and it is better than it sounds because the runny yolk does the job the missing sauce used to do.","(easy) Chopped small and pressed into a toasted sandwich with a slice of mild cheese — the fat is already there, so it needs no butter, and it crisps in the pan beautifully.","(medium) Stirred through fried rice at the last minute, off the heat, with the rice fried in a spoonful of the set coconut fat lifted off the top of the container.","(creative) Shredded with two forks, mixed with a little of its own fat, and rolled into small pastry parcels or spring roll wrappers to fry — the classic martabak-style filling, and the best possible use for the last dry spoonfuls at the bottom of the dish."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Rendang Ayam — Chicken (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":63,"howThisFeels":"Done inside ninety minutes instead of three hours, and the smell hits the same note about halfway through.","trivia":"Chicken rendang is not a lesser substitute invented for people in a hurry — rendang ayam is a standard of the Padang repertoire in its own right, and in much of Malaysia it is the more common of the two. What changes is the arithmetic of time. Chicken has far less collagen than beef chuck and nothing that needs hours to dissolve, so if you simmer it for three hours it disintegrates into threads long before the coconut milk has reduced. The trick is to reduce the liquid first and add the meat to a pot that is already most of the way there.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g beef chuck","to":"160g chicken thighs"},{"from":"160ml coconut milk","to":"130ml coconut milk"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Change the order, because chicken cannot take three hours. Use 160g of chicken thighs, bone out, skin on if you like, cut into pieces about 4cm — thighs and never breast, because breast is lean and goes to cotton wool in a reducing sauce. Make the bumbu and the kerisik exactly as described. Then put the bumbu and the 130ml of coconut milk into the wide pan WITHOUT the meat and simmer it, uncovered, stirring often, for about 25 minutes until it has thickened into a loose golden paste. Now add the chicken and the tamarind, and simmer for 35 to 40 minutes until the chicken is fully tender. Stir in the kerisik and the salt and carry on over low heat, stirring, until the break comes and the oil separates — 10 to 15 minutes at most, because there is much less water to lose. Take it off within about five minutes of the break. Chicken rendang wants to stay just a little glossier and less dry than the beef, or the meat goes stringy while you wait for a crust."}]}},{"name":"Rendang Daging — the Full Dry Rendang","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":74,"howThisFeels":"Three hours in the same room as it, doing almost nothing, and then twenty minutes of not being able to leave the stove.","trivia":"There is a real argument in West Sumatra about how far to take it, and the two ends have different names. Rendang kering — dry rendang — is taken to almost black, with the paste welded onto the meat, and it is the version that keeps for weeks and travels. Rendang basah, wet rendang, is stopped shortly after the break while a little dark sauce still clings. What is remarkable is that a single pot passes through both, plus kalio before them, so three named dishes are separated by nothing but the hour at which you take the pan off the heat. Cooks who grew up with it do not use a clock or a thermometer for any of the three; they read the oil."},{"name":"Rendang Tempe — Fermented Soy Bean Cake (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":89,"howThisFeels":"Chewy, savoury and unexpectedly meaty, with the toasted coconut tasting even louder without beef in the way.","trivia":"Rendang tempe sits on the counter of Padang restaurants alongside the beef, and not as a concession — it is one of the standard cheap everyday rendangs, along with rendang jackfruit and rendang egg. Tempe suits the method for a structural reason: it is whole soya beans bound by a living mould into a firm cake, so it holds its shape in a long reduction where tofu would collapse. It is also porous in a way meat is not, which means it drinks the bumbu instead of merely being coated by it. Because there is no collagen to dissolve and no fat to render, the whole thing is finished in under an hour.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g beef chuck","to":"120g tempeh"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the tempe first, which is the one place this version does sear. Cut 120g of tempeh into slabs about 1cm thick and then into 3cm pieces, and shallow-fry them in a little oil until deep gold on both sides, 3 to 4 minutes a side. Do this before anything else and set them aside. The frying seals the outside so the pieces survive an hour of stirring, and it takes the raw, faintly grassy edge off the mould that a wet braise leaves in place. Then make the bumbu and kerisik as described, and reduce the bumbu with the coconut milk and tamarind in the wide pan on its own, uncovered, stirring, for about 25 minutes, until it is a thick golden paste. Add the fried tempe and the kerisik and the salt and keep going on low heat, stirring often and gently so the pieces do not break up, for 15 to 20 minutes until the break comes and the oil runs clear. Take it off about five minutes after that. Tempe absorbs salt much faster than beef, so taste before you add the full 4g."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥗"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","name":"Sambal Terasi","nameAlt":"Sambal Terasi — chillies and shallots charred dry in a pan, then crushed rather than blended, because a blender makes foam and a pestle makes sambal","aliases":["Sambal Terasi","Sambal Belacan","Indonesian Chilli Sambal","Sambal Bakar","Sambal Bawang","Sambal Matah","Balinese Raw Sambal"],"course":"staple","type":["staple","condiment","sambal","spicy","no-cook","quick","budget"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","braai","guests","make-ahead"],"ingredients":"30g red chilli · 20g shallots · 10g tomato · 5g garlic · 3g shrimp paste · 3g jaggery · 10ml lime juice · 2g salt","method":"Understand what you are making before you start, because sambal is not a hot sauce. A hot sauce is a liquid you add to food. Sambal is a coarse, crushed paste that sits on the side of the plate and is taken in tiny amounts with each mouthful, and every part of the method below exists to keep it coarse. Get it smooth and you have made something else.\n\nFirst, deal with the 3g of shrimp paste, because raw terasi is the single most common reason a homemade sambal tastes wrong. It is fermented krill, pressed into a dark solid block, and straight out of the packet it is aggressive and faintly soapy in a way that does not cook out later. It has to be toasted. Wrap the 3g in a small square of foil, flatten it, and put it in a dry pan over medium heat for about 2 minutes a side, or hold it over a gas flame in a spoon. You will know it is ready by smell rather than sight: the sharp ammonia edge drops away and what is left smells savoury, roasted, somewhere between anchovy and hard cheese. The block also becomes brittle, so it crumbles between your fingers instead of smearing. Toast it in foil rather than loose unless you want the whole house to smell of it for a day, which is a real risk and the reason most Indonesian kitchens do this step outside.\n\nNow char the vegetables, dry, with no oil at all. Put a heavy frying pan over medium-high heat until it is properly hot. Put in the 30g of red chilli whole and unslit, the 20g of shallots peeled and halved, the 5g of garlic in whole unpeeled cloves and the 10g of tomato in a thick slice. Leave them alone for a couple of minutes at a time and turn them only occasionally, until each one is blistered and blackened in patches and gone soft — about 6 to 8 minutes for the lot. Garlic left in its skin is deliberate: the skin insulates the clove so it steams sweet inside while the outside chars, and naked garlic in a dry hot pan turns bitter in well under a minute. Slip the skins off afterwards. The chillies will hiss and pop and may spit a seed at you.\n\nThe charring is doing two separate things and both matter. It takes the raw green sting out of the chilli, which is a harsh, thin heat, and replaces it with a rounder, smokier one, so the sambal tastes hot rather than merely sharp. And it drives water out of the shallot and the tomato while browning their sugars, which is what stops the finished paste being watery and gives it a faint sweetness with no sugar involved. A raw sambal is a real thing, and it is version three below, but it tastes nothing like this one.\n\nNow crush it, and USE A MORTAR AND PESTLE IF YOU HAVE ONE. This is not nostalgia. A blender blade spins and cuts, which shears the cell walls into a fine purée and whips air through it at the same time; the result is a pale pink, frothy, slightly bitter cream that oxidises to a duller colour within an hour. A pestle presses and tears, which bursts the cells and releases the oils without aerating anything, and it leaves flecks of skin and seed intact so the paste has texture and colour depth. If you only have a machine, use short pulses and stop while it is still visibly rough — never let it run.\n\nStart with the salt and the hard things: put the 2g of salt, the toasted crumbled terasi, the charred garlic and the 3g of jaggery into the mortar and work them into a rough paste. The salt is doing double duty here as an abrasive; it gives the pestle something to grind against and breaks the garlic down far faster than pounding alone. Then add the chillies and pound them in. Then the shallots and the tomato last, working them in with a scraping, rolling motion rather than hammering, until you have a thick, uneven, deep red-brown paste with recognisable pieces still in it.\n\nJaggery instead of the palm sugar you would use in Java is the honest local route, and it is a good one. Gula jawa is the dark, soft, slightly smoky sugar pressed from coconut or sugar-palm sap, and jaggery — pressed from cane or palm sap in much the same way, sold in blocks in every Indian grocer in the country — carries the same molasses depth that plain white sugar cannot. Three grams is not there to sweeten the sambal. It is there to round off the sharp corner of the chilli, and you would notice its absence long before you noticed its presence.\n\nStir in the 10ml of lime juice at the very end, off the heat and off the pestle. Acid added earlier fights the charring and dulls the colour; added last it lifts everything and makes the heat taste brighter. Taste it now, on a piece of plain bread or rice rather than off your finger, because neat sambal will simply overwhelm your palate and tell you nothing useful. It should be salty, hot, faintly sweet and faintly funky, in that order, and it should taste like too much on its own — that is correct. On the plate, in a half-teaspoon at a time, it will be exactly right.","cookTime":"Toast 4 min · Char 8 min · Pound 6 min","kcal":"~45 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 2g, Carbs 7g, Fat 0.5g, Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Sambal terasi keeps far better than it looks like it should, for the same reason pickles do — it is salty, acidic and made almost entirely of things that resist spoilage. In a clean sealed jar in the fridge it holds 2 weeks comfortably, and the flavour settles and deepens over the first two or three days as the terasi works through it. Two rules make the difference. Press it down flat in the jar so there is no trapped air, and cover the surface with a thin film of oil if you want a month rather than a fortnight; the oil is a seal, not an ingredient. And always take it out with a clean dry spoon — a wet spoon or a spoon that has been in your food introduces the water and starch that grow mould, and a jar of sambal almost never spoils on its own. It also freezes usefully in an ice cube tray, one cube being roughly a family serving, which is the most efficient way to make a big batch. Do not water-bath or preserve it at room temperature; it has nowhere near enough acid for that, whatever a recipe online says.","chefNotes":"Toast the terasi in foil before anything else, or the sambal will taste soapy no matter what you do next. Char the chillies, shallots, garlic and tomato dry, with the garlic left in its skin. Then crush, do not blend — the texture is the dish. Lime juice goes in last, off the heat.","pairsWith":"Everything, which is the point — sambal is the one thing an Indonesian table is never without. It belongs next to rendang, next to fried tempe, next to plain rice and a fried egg, and it is spectacular on grilled fish and on anything off a braai. A spoonful stirred into mayonnaise makes the best possible chip dip. Keep it alongside something cooling on the same plate — cucumber, or the plain rice itself — because sambal is designed to be eaten against a blank, not on its own.","trivia":"The word sambal is older than the chilli in it, which is the genuinely surprising part. Chillies are American plants; they reached the Indonesian archipelago with Portuguese and Spanish traders in the 1500s, moving through Malacca and Manila, and they were so immediately at home in the cooking that within a couple of generations nobody thought of them as foreign. But heat had been part of the food for centuries before they arrived. It came from cabya, the Javanese long pepper, from black pepper — which the archipelago grew and sold to the world — and from ginger and galangal. The oldest Javanese references to a pounded chilli-and-shallot relish under the name sambal come from around the 1700s, by which time the imported plant had simply moved into a slot that was already there and pushed the long pepper out of it. Cabya is now so obscure in Indonesia that it is grown mostly as a medicinal curiosity.\n\nWhat followed is one of the more extreme cases of regional specialisation anywhere in food. Indonesia has hundreds of named sambals, and they are not variations on a theme so much as genuinely different objects: sambal matah from Bali is raw and sliced, not pounded at all; sambal ijo from Padang is made with green chillies and is pale and mild-looking and is not; sambal dabu-dabu from Manado is closer to a fresh salsa; sambal pecel is a solid dried brick you reconstitute. Terasi itself is worth a note. It is krill or tiny shrimp salted and fermented for weeks and then pressed, and it exists in a family that runs the length of the region under different names — belacan in Malaysia, kapi in Thailand, bagoong in the Philippines — and in the same evolutionary slot as the Roman garum and the fish sauces of Vietnam. It is one of the oldest tricks in cooking: let protein break itself down with salt and time, and you get a concentrated savouriness that no fresh ingredient can give you.","howThisFeels":"The pop and hiss of whole chillies blistering in a dry pan, and then the particular scraping rhythm of a pestle that means somebody in the house is about to eat properly.","leftovers":["(easy) A teaspoon stirred through hot rice with nothing else is a real meal in Indonesia and a genuinely good one — nasi sambal, eaten standing up.","(easy) Beaten into softened butter and rolled in baking paper into a log, chilled, then sliced onto a hot steak or corn on the cob straight off the braai.","(medium) Loosened with a little water and lime and used as a marinade for chicken thighs or fish before grilling — a heaped tablespoon per 500g, an hour is enough, and the sugar in it means it will catch, so keep it away from the hottest part of the fire.","(creative) Fried briefly in oil with extra shallots and a splash of tamarind until it darkens into sambal goreng, the cooked keeping-sambal, which turns a fortnight in the fridge into two months and tastes entirely different — deeper, sweeter, less bright."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Sambal Bawang — Chilli and Shallot Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":9,"howThisFeels":"Four things in a bowl and hot oil poured over them, and it is on the table in five minutes.","trivia":"Sambal bawang is the sambal of warungs and fried chicken stalls, and it is made in enormous quantities because it takes almost nothing and almost no time. There is one technique in it and it is worth having: the raw pounded chilli and shallot are finished by pouring smoking hot oil straight over them rather than by frying them. The oil hitting the paste flash-cooks the surface of everything at once, which knocks out the raw sting in a couple of seconds without any of the browning a pan would give, so the sambal stays a bright screaming red and tastes fresh and violent rather than smoky and round. It is the same trick as a Chinese chilli oil, arrived at independently.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"10g tomato"},{"item":"3g shrimp paste"},{"item":"3g jaggery"}],"addIng":[{"item":"15ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the toasting and skip the charring entirely. Pound the 30g of red chilli, the 20g of shallots, the 5g of garlic and the 2g of salt together RAW in the mortar, into a rough wet paste with visible pieces still in it, and scrape it into a small heatproof bowl. Heat the 15ml of sunflower oil in a small pan until it is genuinely smoking — a scrap of shallot dropped in should fizz hard immediately — and pour it straight over the paste. It will hiss and foam up and the smell will change in about two seconds from raw onion to something cooked. Stir it through, add the lime juice, and leave it five minutes. Taste before adding more salt; the hot oil concentrates everything."}]}},{"name":"Sambal Terasi Bakar — Charred, with Shrimp Paste","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":10,"howThisFeels":"Blackened blistered chilli skins going into the mortar, and a colour in the bowl at the end that no jar in a shop has ever managed.","trivia":"Bakar means grilled or burnt, and the charring is not a garnish step but the entire difference between this and the raw sambals. There is chemistry in it that a home cook can taste directly. Blistering the skin of a chilli in dry heat drives off water and starts browning the sugars in the flesh, which is why a charred sambal tastes sweeter and deeper even though nothing sweet has been added, and why it needs so little sugar to balance. In West Sumatra and Java the same job is often done in the ashes of a wood fire rather than a pan, which is worth trying on a braai the next time the coals are dying — bury the chillies and whole shallots in the hot ash at the edge for ten minutes and the sambal comes out with a smokiness a stove cannot reproduce."},{"name":"Sambal Matah — Bali's Raw Sliced Sambal (Vegan)","icon":"🌿","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":13,"howThisFeels":"Sharp, crunchy and startlingly fresh — closer to a fierce salad than to anything you would call a sauce.","trivia":"Matah means raw, and sambal matah breaks the two rules the others keep: nothing is cooked and nothing is pounded. Everything is sliced as finely as a knife allows and then bruised together by hand with salt and hot oil. The reason is textural — matah is meant to crunch — and the reason it works at all is the lemongrass, which is fibrous and inedible in most raw preparations but becomes wonderful sliced paper-thin across the grain. It is a Balinese dish and Bali is the Hindu island in a mostly Muslim country, which shows up in its food in a hundred small ways; the classic matah is finished with coconut oil rather than a neutral one, and it belongs beside grilled fish more than beside anything braised.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"10g tomato"},{"item":"3g shrimp paste"},{"item":"3g jaggery"}],"addIng":[{"item":"8g lemongrass"},{"item":"15ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Nothing is cooked and nothing is pounded, so the knife work is the whole dish. Slice the 20g of shallots into the thinnest possible rings, slice the 30g of red chilli finely across so it comes off in little rings too, mince the 5g of garlic, and take the 8g of lemongrass down to the pale bottom third and slice it across the grain as fine as you can physically manage — this is the one non-negotiable cut, because lemongrass sliced thick is woody splinters and lemongrass sliced fine is the best thing in the bowl. Put it all in a bowl with the 2g of salt and squeeze and rub it between your fingers for a minute until it wilts slightly and smells enormous; the salt and the bruising are standing in for cooking. Heat the 15ml of sunflower oil until just smoking and pour it over, stir, then add the lime juice. If you can get a kaffir lime leaf, roll it into a cigar and shred it in hairline strips as well. Eat it the same day — matah is a fresh thing and it goes flat and dull by tomorrow, which is exactly the trade for the crunch."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥘"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥗"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","name":"Tempe Goreng","nameAlt":"Tempe Goreng — soya beans held together by a living mould, brined in turmeric and coriander water and then fried until the outside snaps","aliases":["Tempe Goreng","Tempeh Goreng","Fried Tempeh","Tempe Bumbu Kuning","Tempe Kering","Kering Tempe","Tempe Mendoan","Sambal Goreng Tempe"],"course":"side","type":["side","snack","fried","fermented","vegan","protein","budget","quick"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","lunchbox","guests","meat-free"],"ingredients":"120g tempeh · 50ml water · 40ml sunflower oil · 5g garlic · 4g coriander seeds · 3g ground turmeric · 3g salt","method":"Look at the block before you cut it, because tempe tells you plainly what condition it is in and it is the one ingredient here that can be wrong. A good block is firm enough to pick up in one hand without bending, dense, with the individual soya beans clearly visible through a white felt of mycelium that binds them into a solid cake. It should smell of mushrooms and fresh bread and very slightly of the beans themselves. Grey or black patches, usually around the pinholes in the wrapper or along an edge, are NOT spoilage — that is the mould sporulating where it found air, it is entirely normal, and those patches simply taste stronger and nuttier. What you do not want is sliminess, a strong ammonia smell, or any colour that is not white, grey or black. Yellow, pink or green means something else moved in and the block goes in the bin.\n\nCut the 120g of tempe on a slight diagonal into slices about 5mm thick. The angle is not decoration; it gives you a longer face for the same weight, which means more crust per piece. And 5mm is the deliberate middle of a real range: at 3mm the slices go crisp all the way through like a chip and lose the soft nutty interior, at 10mm you keep a creamy middle but the outside will never properly snap. Five is where you get both.\n\nNow the brine, which is the step people skip and which is doing more than seasoning. Crush the 5g of garlic, the 4g of coriander seeds, the 3g of ground turmeric and the 3g of salt together into a rough paste — mortar, or the flat of a knife — and stir it into the 50ml of water in a shallow bowl. This is bumbu kuning, yellow paste, in its simplest form, and every Indonesian household has some version of it. Lay the tempe slices in the liquid in a single layer and leave them 15 to 20 minutes, turning them once.\n\nWhat that does is specific. Tempe is porous in a way meat is not — it is thousands of whole beans held in a mesh with air between them — so it drinks a thin salted liquid all the way to the centre in a quarter of an hour. Rub a dry spice paste on the outside instead and you get a seasoned crust wrapped around bland beans, which is the difference between tempe that tastes of something and tempe people describe as cardboard. Coriander seed is the signature: warm, faintly citrus, and it is in nearly every Javanese bumbu for a reason. Turmeric is doing colour and a slight bitterness that cuts the richness of the fry.\n\nThen DRY THE SLICES PROPERLY before they go anywhere near the pan. Lift them out, let them drain a moment and pat both faces firmly with a cloth or kitchen paper until the surface is no longer wet to the touch. Wet tempe in hot oil does two bad things: it spits violently, and the water on the surface has to boil off before any browning can start, which means the inside overcooks while the outside is still pale. You want the interior of the slice damp and the exterior dry.\n\nHeat the 40ml of sunflower oil in a frying pan over medium heat. This is a shallow fry, not a deep one — about 3 to 4mm of oil, enough that the slices sit in it rather than on it. Get it to a moderate 170°C or thereabouts. Test it with a corner of a slice: it should fizz steadily and continuously, not explode and not sit there quietly. Moderate heat matters here more than in most frying. Too hot and the outside browns in forty seconds while the beans inside are still chalky and taste raw and beany; the mould and the starch in the beans both need a little time in real heat to turn nutty. Too cool and the slices absorb oil and go greasy and limp.\n\nFry in a single layer with space between the slices, 3 to 4 minutes a side, turning once. Do it in two batches rather than crowding the pan — crowded slices drop the oil temperature, steam each other and never crisp. They are done when they are a deep even gold, the edges have gone slightly darker and a little frilled, and a slice tapped with a spoon sounds hard rather than soft. Take them out onto a rack rather than onto paper if you can, because paper traps steam under the bottom face and softens the crust you just made.\n\nSalt them lightly the moment they come out of the oil, while the surface is still hot enough to hold it, and eat them within about ten minutes. Fried tempe is at its best almost immediately, then goes progressively chewier as the moisture inside works back out to the surface, and while it is still good half an hour later it is not the same thing. If you need to hold it, keep it in a single layer in a warm oven with the door slightly open — never covered, never stacked.","cookTime":"Brine 20 min · Fry 8 min","kcal":"~330 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 21g, Carbs 12g, Fat 22g, Sodium ~700mg","storage":"The uncooked block is the part worth being careful with, because tempe is a live culture and it does not stop when you buy it. In the fridge the mould slows but keeps working, which means the block goes progressively darker, softer and stronger-smelling over about 5 to 7 days — not spoiled, but heading past what most people want. Wrap it and use it inside a week. It freezes extremely well for 3 months and the freezer is genuinely the right home for it, because freezing stops the culture dead and the texture afterwards is barely changed; slice it before freezing so you can take out what you need. Thaw in the fridge, then dry the slices well since they will weep a little. Fried tempe is a different matter and does not keep in any satisfying way — it will sit in the fridge for 3 days and reheats respectably in a hot oven or air fryer for 4 to 5 minutes, but it never comes back to that first crispness. The exception is the sweet crisp version in the second variation below, which is deliberately fried past all its moisture and keeps for weeks in a jar, and that is not an accident but the entire reason it exists.","chefNotes":"Slice on the diagonal at 5mm — thinner loses the soft middle, thicker never snaps. Brine the slices in turmeric, coriander, garlic and salt water for twenty minutes so the seasoning reaches the centre, then dry both faces properly before frying. Moderate oil at about 170°C, single layer, two batches, and out onto a rack rather than paper.","pairsWith":"Plain white rice, sambal and a spoonful of something braised is the classic plate, and fried tempe alongside rendang gives that meal the crunch it is missing. It is also very good simply as a snack with sambal to dip into, and it is the thing that turns gado-gado from a salad into a meal. A wedge of lime over the hot slices does more than you would expect.","trivia":"Tempe is unusual among the world's great fermented foods, and the reason is structural rather than cultural. Almost every other fermented soya product — miso, doenjang, soy sauce, douchi — is made by breaking the beans down into a paste or a liquid. Tempe leaves them whole and binds them together instead, with the mycelium of a mould called Rhizopus oligosporus, which grows through the mass in a day or two at around 30°C and knits it into a sliceable cake. The mould has to be given a clean start to do it, which is why the beans are soaked in acidified water first: the acid keeps competing bacteria out for long enough for the Rhizopus to take the territory. In that day or two the mould's own enzymes do a real amount of work — proteases that break long soya proteins into shorter, more digestible pieces and free amino acids, lipases that release fatty acids, and phytase that dismantles a chunk of the phytate that otherwise blocks the absorption of iron and zinc. Tempe is not simply soaked soya beans in a new shape; it is soya beans that have already been partly digested for you, which is why it is easier on the stomach than the beans it came from and why it tastes savoury rather than merely beany.\n\nTwo footnotes that are worth having straight. The famous claim that tempe is a vegan source of vitamin B12 is half true and the half that is false matters. The mould makes no B12 at all. What made traditional Indonesian tempe a B12 source was uninvited bacterial company, principally Citrobacter freundii and Klebsiella pneumoniae, living alongside the Rhizopus in open village production; tempe made in a clean modern facility, or at home with a pure starter, contains little or none. Anyone relying on it for B12 should read the label rather than the folklore. And the second: Javanese cooking makes deliberate use of tempe taken well past the point most people would call fresh. Tempe semangit, sometimes tempe bosok, is left until it is dark, soft and pungent, and it is then used in small quantities as a seasoning in dishes like sayur lodeh and sambal tumpang — the same logic as a blue cheese or an aged anchovy. The oldest clear written reference to tempe by name appears in the Serat Centhini, a long Javanese poem committed to paper in Surakarta around 1814, though the food is certainly older than the manuscript.","howThisFeels":"The particular dry snap of the first slice between your teeth, and then the soft nutty middle behind it that no vegetable and no meat quite tastes like.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold fried tempe, chopped, is excellent through any salad and needs nothing but a squeeze of lime — it goes chewy rather than soggy, which is why it survives dressing.","(easy) Crumbled into fried rice at the last second, off the heat, so it keeps a little of its edge.","(medium) Chopped small and simmered ten minutes in coconut milk with a spoonful of sambal into a quick sayur — the slices go soft and drink the sauce, which is a genuinely different dish rather than reheated leftovers.","(creative) Blitzed coarse with garlic, chilli and lime into a thick spread for bread or roti, which is roughly what a Javanese cook does with tempe that has gone too soft to fry neatly."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Tempe Bumbu Kuning — Plain Brined and Fried (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":45,"howThisFeels":"Twenty minutes of waiting and eight of frying, and it is the cheapest properly satisfying protein in the house.","trivia":"This is the everyday form and the one sold from carts by the bag, and its economics are worth stating: soya beans and a mould culture turn into a firm, sliceable, high-protein food with no animal in the chain and almost no equipment. A block of tempe carries roughly a fifth of its weight as protein, comparable to a lean meat, and the fermentation makes more of that protein absorbable rather than less. In Java it has been the cheap protein of the countryside for centuries, which is why the phrase for a poor household's meal is rice, tempe and sambal, and why the price of soya beans is a political matter there in the way the price of bread is elsewhere."},{"name":"Tempe Kering Manis — Sweet Crisp Matchsticks","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":50,"howThisFeels":"Sticky, hot, salty and sweet all at once, and impossible to stop picking at while it cools on the tray.","trivia":"Kering means dry, and this version is deliberately fried past the point of tenderness until every bit of moisture is gone — which is the whole reason it exists. Dry tempe sticks turned in a caramel keep for weeks in a jar at room temperature, so a household could fry a big batch and eat off it, and it travels in a lunch tin without turning soggy. It is one of the standard components of a Padang or a rice-parcel plate for exactly that reason. The technique point is the caramel: the sugar syrup must be taken to the sticky, thread-forming stage BEFORE the tempe goes in, and the tempe must be fully cold and fully crisp when it does, or the moisture in the sticks dissolves the syrup and the whole batch turns tacky instead of shattering.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"15g jaggery"},{"item":"10g red chilli"},{"item":"5g tamarind paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cut and fry differently, then make a caramel. Cut the 120g of tempe into matchsticks about 4mm square rather than slices — thin enough to dry out completely. Brine them exactly as described but only for 10 minutes, since there is far more surface for the same weight, and dry them very well. Fry them in the 40ml of oil over medium heat for 6 to 8 minutes, stirring, until they are deep gold, rigid and the bubbling in the pan has gone noticeably quiet; that quiet is the water leaving and it is the doneness cue, because a stick that still bends will be chewy tomorrow. Lift them out and let them cool COMPLETELY on a rack — this is not optional, warm sticks steam and go limp in the syrup. Pour off all but about 10ml of the oil, add the 10g of red chilli finely sliced and fry it 30 seconds, then add the 15g of jaggery, the 5g of tamarind paste and a tablespoon of water. Let it bubble, without stirring more than a swirl, for 2 to 3 minutes until it thickens and a little dripped from the spoon falls in a thread rather than a drop. Take the pan off the heat, tip in the cold tempe sticks and toss fast and hard for about 20 seconds until every stick is glossed. Spread them out on a tray to set, separated, and cool completely before jarring."}]}},{"name":"Tempe Mendoan — Deliberately Half-Fried in Batter","icon":"🌾","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":49,"howThisFeels":"Floppy, pale, hot and completely wrong-looking, and then the best thing on the table with sambal and a cup of tea.","trivia":"Mendoan comes from a Banyumas word meaning half-cooked, and it is one of very few fried foods anywhere that is a mistake made on purpose. The tempe is sliced thin and wide, dipped in a loose spiced batter and lifted out of the oil while the batter is still pale, soft and visibly underdone — no crust, no colour, no snap. It exists because the region eats it fresh from the wok in the rain with sweet tea and a whole green chilli to bite, and a crisp coating would have gone soft on the walk home anyway. There is a real technique behind the floppiness: the batter must be thin enough to cling in a film rather than a jacket, and the oil hot rather than moderate, so the surface sets in well under a minute and comes out before browning has a chance to start.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"30g flour"},{"item":"10g spring onions"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Slice and batter instead of brining. Cut the 120g of tempe into wide slices about 3mm thick — as thin and as broad as the block allows, which is the opposite of the main method. Crush the garlic, coriander, turmeric and salt as described but whisk them into the 50ml of water WITH the 30g of flour and the 10g of spring onions finely sliced, to make a thin batter about the consistency of pouring cream; if it coats the back of a spoon opaquely it is too thick and needs another spoonful of water. Heat the 40ml of oil hotter than for the main version, near 190°C, so a drop of batter sizzles and sets on contact. Dip each slice, let the excess run off for a second, and fry 45 to 60 seconds a side ONLY. Take them out while the batter is still pale and soft with barely a hint of gold at the edges. It will look undercooked and that is the dish. Eat them immediately, straight off the paper, with sambal and, if you are doing it properly, a raw green chilli to bite between mouthfuls."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥘"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥗"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-gado-gado","name":"Gado-Gado","nameAlt":"Gado-Gado — the one salad where every vegetable is cooked separately to its own doneness, then dressed in a peanut sauce you loosen with hot water until it just coats","aliases":["Gado-Gado","Gado Gado","Indonesian Peanut Salad","Pecel","Lotek","Karedok","Indonesian Salad with Peanut Sauce"],"course":"main","type":["main","salad","vegetarian","peanut","meat-free","lunch","street-food"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","lunch","guests","meat-free"],"ingredients":"80g cabbage · 80g firm tofu · 60g green beans · 50g potato · 40g bean sprouts · 40g cucumber · 40g raw peanuts · 1 large egg · 30ml sunflower oil · 100ml water · 15g red chilli · 10g jaggery · 5g garlic · 5g tamarind paste · 3g salt","method":"The single decision that separates good gado-gado from the wet, khaki version people are politely disappointed by is this: COOK EVERY VEGETABLE SEPARATELY, and cook each one only until it is right. It is tempting to put the beans, the cabbage and the sprouts into one pot to save washing up, and it is the whole problem, because green beans need four minutes, shredded cabbage needs ninety seconds and bean sprouts need about fifteen seconds. Cook them together and you are choosing which two to ruin. One pot of boiling salted water, one vegetable at a time, lifted out with a spider or a sieve — the water gets reused, so it is one pot either way.\n\nStart with the 50g of potato, because it is the longest. Peel it, cut it into 2cm pieces and boil until a knife goes in with no resistance, 12 to 15 minutes, then drain and let it steam dry. Next the 60g of green beans, topped and cut into 4cm lengths: 3 to 4 minutes, until they have gone from squeaky raw to just tender with a slight bite left. Then the 80g of cabbage, shredded about 1cm wide: 90 seconds, no more — you want it wilted and still pale green, not grey and floppy. Last the 40g of bean sprouts: 15 to 20 seconds, literally in and out, which is enough to take the raw beany taste away and not nearly enough to lose the snap that they are entirely there for. The 40g of cucumber is never cooked; slice it thickly and leave it raw and cold.\n\nAs each one comes out, spread it on a tray or a cloth in a single layer to cool and DRY. This is the second half of the same principle. Peanut sauce is a coating, not a soup, and a coating cannot cling to a wet surface — the water on a hot drained vegetable thins the sauce exactly where the two touch, and a plate of gado-gado dressed while everything is still steaming turns into a pool within a minute. Room temperature and dry to the touch is the target. Do not rinse anything under cold water to speed this up; you will wash off flavour and add the water you are trying to remove.\n\nFry the 80g of firm tofu while the vegetables cool. Press it first — a plate on top and ten minutes is enough — then cut it into 2cm cubes, pat every face dry, and fry in the 30ml of sunflower oil over medium-high heat until at least two faces of each cube are properly golden and crisp, 6 to 8 minutes in all. Do not move it about for the first two minutes; tofu needs to be left alone long enough to release from the pan on its own, and a cube prodded early tears and sticks. Lift the tofu out and set it aside. Boil the egg in the same pot as the vegetables — 8 minutes from boiling for a just-set yolk that is still slightly glossy in the middle, then straight into cold water, which stops it cooking and makes it peel cleanly. Halve it just before serving.\n\nNow the sauce, and it starts with frying the peanuts rather than boiling them. Put the 40g of raw peanuts into the tofu oil, still over medium heat, and fry them for 4 to 5 minutes, stirring constantly, until they are a shade or two darker and smell strongly toasted. Watch them properly. Peanuts go from perfect to burnt in about twenty seconds and a single scorched batch makes a bitter sauce that nothing can rescue. Lift them out onto a plate to cool and crisp — they will not be crunchy while hot, so do not judge them in the pan.\n\nPound or blitz the cooled peanuts with the 5g of garlic, the 15g of red chilli, the 10g of jaggery, the 5g of tamarind paste and the 3g of salt into a thick, coarse, sandy paste. Leave it slightly rough. A completely smooth sauce tastes flat and slightly gluey, and the small fragments of peanut are a large part of why this dish is satisfying rather than merely pleasant.\n\nThen loosen it with the 100ml of water, HOT, and add it in three or four goes rather than all at once, stirring hard after each. Hot water does two things cold water does not: it melts the jaggery so there is no grittiness, and it emulsifies the peanut oil into the liquid instead of leaving it to separate and float. You are looking for the consistency of thin cream — thick enough that a spoon drawn through it leaves a track for a second, thin enough to pour. It will thicken as it stands, so stop just short and keep the kettle nearby.\n\nTaste it now and adjust it deliberately, because peanut sauce is a four-way balance and every batch of peanuts, tamarind and chilli is different. It should be savoury first, then nutty, then sour, then sweet, with the heat running underneath all of it. Flat and heavy means it needs tamarind. Harsh and thin means it needs jaggery. Dull means salt. Get this right and the dish is right; the vegetables are only ever as good as the sauce on them.\n\nAssemble on a wide plate rather than in a bowl, so the sauce sits on the vegetables instead of collecting under them. Arrange the potato, beans, cabbage, sprouts, cucumber and tofu in loose separate groups, put the halved egg on top, and spoon the sauce over generously without stirring it in. Gado-gado is meant to be mixed by the person eating it — that is what the name is about — and mixing it in the kitchen means the last mouthful tastes exactly like the first, which is the one thing this dish is not for.","cookTime":"Boil vegetables 20 min · Fry tofu 8 min · Sauce 12 min","kcal":"~560 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 27g, Carbs 40g, Fat 33g, Sodium ~820mg","storage":"Store the sauce and the components apart and this becomes one of the best packed lunches there is; store it assembled and it is ruined by morning. The peanut sauce keeps 5 days in a sealed jar in the fridge and thickens to a paste in there, which is normal — loosen it with a splash of hot water and stir hard, and it comes back exactly. It freezes for 3 months, and freezing it in an ice cube tray is worth doing, since two cubes and a kettle is a dressing on a weeknight. The blanched vegetables hold 2 to 3 days in a closed container in the fridge, though the bean sprouts are the exception and go limp within a day, so add those fresh. Fried tofu keeps 3 days and crisps up again in a hot dry pan in two minutes. The boiled egg, unpeeled, holds a week. Assembled gado-gado does not keep at all and should not be refrigerated, because the vegetables weep, the sauce splits and the sprouts collapse; make up only what is going to be eaten.","chefNotes":"One pot, one vegetable at a time — potato 15 minutes, beans 4, cabbage 90 seconds, sprouts 15 seconds — then spread everything out to cool and dry properly before it meets the sauce. Fry the peanuts rather than boiling them, and watch them, because burnt peanuts cannot be fixed. Loosen the sauce with HOT water, a little at a time, to the thickness of thin cream. Spoon it over at the table and let people mix it themselves.","pairsWith":"It is a complete meal on its own, which is unusual for a salad, and the traditional additions are all about texture rather than more food: prawn crackers to scoop with, a spoonful of sambal on the rim for people who want it hotter, and a wedge of lime. A little warm rice or a couple of lontong rice cakes underneath turns it from a large lunch into a proper supper. Fried tempe alongside is the standard Indonesian move and the crunch is welcome.","trivia":"Peanut sauce in Indonesia is not usually made from scratch, and the shortcut is more interesting than the long way. In Java the sauce goes by the name bumbu pecel and it is sold in markets as a solid brick — roasted peanuts, chilli, garlic, kencur, tamarind and palm sugar pounded into a dense block that keeps for weeks unrefrigerated with no packaging technology at all. You break off a piece, pour hot water on it, stir, and you have the sauce. It is a shelf-stable concentrate arrived at centuries before anyone used that phrase, and the same logic as a bouillon cube, except that the brick is the honest version and the cube is not.\n\nThe brick also explains the family this dish belongs to, which is far larger than gado-gado alone. Pecel, the Javanese original, is blanched vegetables with the brick sauce poured over. Lotek, from the Sundanese west, is pounded together with the vegetables in the mortar so the whole thing is half-crushed. Karedok, also Sundanese, uses the vegetables entirely raw. Gado-gado is the Jakarta member of the family, the most cosmopolitan of them, and it is the one that picked up the potato, the egg and the fried tofu — the potato in particular arriving with the Dutch, which is why it is in the capital's version and not in the village ones. The name itself is the giveaway: gado means to eat something as a mixed mouthful with no rice under it, and doubling a word in Indonesian and Malay makes it plural, casual or repetitive. Gado-gado is roughly mix-mix, or a bit of this and a bit of that — a name that describes an eating action rather than a recipe, which is precisely why nobody agrees on what goes in it and why arranging it in neat separate piles and letting the eater wreck them is the correct way to serve it.","howThisFeels":"Cold cucumber, warm potato, a still-soft egg yolk and a sauce thick enough to hold all of it together — the rare plate where nothing on it is the same temperature and that turns out to be the point.","leftovers":["(easy) Leftover peanut sauce thinned a little further is the best noodle dressing in the house — hot noodles, sauce, sliced spring onion, done in four minutes.","(easy) The blanched vegetables reheat into a fast soup with any stock and a spoonful of the sauce stirred in at the end off the heat.","(medium) Peanut sauce brushed over chicken thighs or aubergine slices and grilled — keep it away from fierce heat because the jaggery in it catches quickly, and brush it on in the last few minutes rather than the first.","(creative) Sauce, cold vegetables and the fried tofu rolled into rice paper or a wrap with fresh herbs, which makes something closer to a summer roll and is better cold than the original ever is."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Pecel — Vegetables and Sauce Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"Cheap, green, hot with chilli and startlingly filling for a plate with nothing rich on it.","trivia":"Pecel is the older and plainer parent of gado-gado and it is what most of Java actually eats — blanched vegetables and the peanut sauce, served on a folded banana leaf, often for breakfast. What is worth noticing is that stripping out the tofu and the egg does not make it a lesser meal nutritionally, because peanuts and the vegetables between them still deliver a decent protein load; it makes it a cheaper one. In Javanese markets pecel is also the standard use for whatever greens are going: water spinach, papaya leaf, cassava leaf, bean sprouts and long beans in whatever combination the morning produced, which is a far better principle for a home cook than a fixed ingredient list.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"80g firm tofu"},{"item":"1 large egg"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"30ml sunflower oil","to":"20ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the tofu and the egg entirely and use the 20ml of oil purely to fry the peanuts. Everything else is unchanged, but since there is now nothing rich on the plate, increase the vegetables rather than the sauce — another 40g of whatever green you have, blanched to its own timing, keeps this a meal rather than a side. This is also the version to use whatever is in the fridge on: spinach, morogo, kale, broccoli, water spinach and long beans all belong here, each cooked separately as described. Serve it slightly warm rather than cold, which is how it is eaten in Java, and put the sambal on the side rather than in the sauce."}]}},{"name":"Gado-Gado — with Fried Tofu and Egg","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"A plate with six separate things on it in neat piles, and the small satisfaction of destroying the arrangement with a spoon.","trivia":"The two additions that make this the Jakarta version tell you something about the city. The potato is Dutch — a colonial import that took hold in the capital's cooking and never reached the village pecel — and the fried tofu is Chinese, from the same Peranakan kitchens that gave the archipelago its noodles and its spring rolls. Gado-gado is a colonial port's salad in the most literal sense: a Javanese sauce over a Javanese method with a Dutch tuber and a Chinese protein on top. The egg is the newest arrival of the three and the most negotiable; plenty of Jakarta warungs still serve it without."},{"name":"Karedok — Raw, the Sundanese Way (Vegan)","icon":"🌿","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":46,"howThisFeels":"Loud, cold and crunchy from the first bite to the last, with none of the softness a cooked salad has.","trivia":"Karedok is the West Javanese version and nothing in it is cooked at all — raw long beans, raw cabbage, raw bean sprouts, cucumber and Thai basil, with the peanut sauce pounded in a mortar and the vegetables tossed straight into it. It works because Sundanese food is built around raw vegetables in a way the rest of Indonesian cooking is not; a Sundanese meal often begins with lalapan, a plate of raw leaves and cucumber with sambal, before anything cooked appears. The signature aromatic in a proper karedok sauce is kencur, a small aromatic ginger with a sharp medicinal edge that is not stocked in South Africa. Fresh ginger with a little extra lime is the honest stand-in — it is not the same note, and it is far better than leaving the sauce flat.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"50g potato"},{"item":"1 large egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"8g fresh ginger"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cook none of the vegetables. Slice the 60g of green beans very thinly on the diagonal — thin is doing the work that blanching does in the main version, and beans cut in 4cm lengths raw are unpleasant while beans cut in 3mm slices are excellent. Shred the 80g of cabbage as finely as you can, leave the 40g of bean sprouts and the 40g of cucumber entirely as they are, and keep everything cold. Fry the tofu and the peanuts as described. Pound the 8g of fresh ginger in with the peanuts, garlic, chilli, jaggery, tamarind and salt, then loosen with the hot water as before but stop a little thicker — nothing here is going to release water into it. Toss the raw vegetables through the sauce in a bowl rather than plating them separately, since the sauce needs to reach the inside of the shredded cabbage. Eat it within the half hour: raw cabbage and sprouts sitting in a salted dressing wilt and weep, and karedok that has waited an hour is just a wet salad."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥘"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","name":"Nasi Uduk","nameAlt":"Nasi Uduk — coconut rice cooked in two stages, absorbed then steamed, because coconut milk on a straight boil will scorch the bottom of the pot before the grains are done","aliases":["Nasi Uduk","Nasi Uduk Betawi","Indonesian Coconut Rice","Nasi Lemak","Nasi Kuning","Yellow Rice","Coconut Rice"],"course":"staple","type":["staple","rice","side","breakfast","vegan","budget","festive"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","breakfast","celebration","guests"],"ingredients":"90g white rice · 100ml coconut milk · 80ml water · 12g lemongrass · 8g galangal · 2 bay leaves · 3g salt","method":"Rinse the 90g of rice, and stop before you have rinsed it too much. Put it in a bowl, cover it with cold water, swirl it with your hand until the water goes milky, pour it off and repeat — three or four times, until the water is cloudy rather than white. What you are washing away is loose surface starch, which is what makes rice claggy and makes separate grains stick into a mass. What you are NOT trying to do is rinse it perfectly clear; that takes eight or nine changes, it strips a little of the outside of each grain, and coconut rice in particular wants a small amount of that starch left behind to give it body. Drain it properly in a sieve for a couple of minutes so you are not adding unmeasured water to a measured liquid.\n\nBruise the aromatics rather than chopping them. Take the 12g of lemongrass, trim it to the pale bottom third, and whack it two or three times along its length with the flat of a heavy knife or the base of a pan until it splits and frays and you can smell it across the kitchen. Do the same to the 8g of galangal, sliced into two or three thick coins first. Bruising ruptures the oil cells so the aromatics release into the liquid, while leaving the pieces big enough to pull out at the end — chop them fine and you get woody splinters through the rice that nobody can pick out. The 2 bay leaves go in whole.\n\nA word about the bay, because honesty is better than a substitution nobody mentions. The leaf in a Jakarta kitchen is daun salam, from a tree unrelated to the Mediterranean bay, and it is milder, slightly more tannic and less eucalyptus-sharp. European bay is the accepted stand-in and it works, but use two leaves and not four; the same quantity that would be right for daun salam will push a bay-leaf note over the coconut and make the rice taste faintly of stew.\n\nNow the part that is the whole recipe. Put the drained rice, the 100ml of coconut milk, the 80ml of water, the aromatics and the 3g of salt into a heavy pot with a tight lid, and note that the liquid is DILUTED — 100ml of coconut milk and 80ml of water, not 180ml of coconut milk. Cooking rice in undiluted coconut milk sounds like it would be better and it is not: the fat coats the grains and slows water absorption so the centres stay hard, and the result is greasy rather than fragrant. Diluting is not economising. It is how the dish is made.\n\nBring it to a boil uncovered, stirring gently once or twice, then turn the heat right down to the lowest it will go, put the lid on, and cook for about 10 to 12 minutes — until the liquid is fully absorbed and the surface of the rice is pitted with small craters where steam has pushed through. This stage has a name in Indonesian, mengaron, and the half-cooked rice it produces is called aron. Lift the lid and check by dragging a spoon to the bottom at the edge: there should be no free liquid at all, and the grains should still be firm in the middle, not done.\n\nWHY IT IS DONE IN TWO STAGES, and this is the thing worth carrying to every other coconut rice you ever make: coconut milk is not water. It carries fat, protein and a little sugar, and all three of them catch on the base of a hot pot. Boiled all the way through, the bottom layer scorches and goes bitter well before the top layer is cooked, and one burnt patch flavours the whole pot. So the coconut milk is used only for the absorption stage, where there is liquid in the pot protecting the base, and the finish is done in steam, where nothing is touching hot metal. This is why Indonesian cooks move the aron into a steamer, and it is why the many recipes that simply boil rice in coconut milk from start to finish produce either a burnt bottom or an undercooked middle.\n\nSo now steam it. The easiest version at home is to leave it in the same pot: keep the lid on, keep the heat at its absolute lowest, and give it another 12 to 15 minutes without lifting the lid once. The trapped steam finishes the grains from the outside in, gently, with no direct contact. If your pot is thin or your smallest flame is still fierce, use a proper steamer — tip the aron into a steamer basket lined with a cloth or baking paper, spread it loosely rather than packed, and steam over boiling water for 15 minutes. That is the traditional method and it is genuinely more forgiving.\n\nThen take it off the heat and LEAVE IT ALONE, lid on, for 10 minutes. This is not resting for the sake of resting. Rice comes off the heat with a moisture gradient — wetter at the bottom, drier at the top — and ten minutes of standing lets that even out through the whole pot. Fluffed immediately, the top is dry and the bottom is wet; fluffed after ten minutes, all of it is the same.\n\nFish out the lemongrass, galangal and bay, then fluff with a FORK, lifting from the bottom of the pot and turning the rice over rather than stirring it. A spoon presses and drags, which crushes cooked grains against each other and releases the starch you carefully rinsed off at the start. A fork separates. The finished rice should smell strongly of coconut, sit in distinct grains, and taste seasoned on its own — if it needs salt at the table, it needed more salt in the pot.","cookTime":"Rinse 5 min · Absorb 12 min · Steam 15 min · Rest 10 min","kcal":"~430 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 7g, Carbs 74g, Fat 12g, Sodium ~1150mg","storage":"Coconut rice needs a little more care than plain rice, and the reason is the fat rather than the rice. Cool it fast and uncovered — spread it out on a tray rather than leaving a warm pot on the stove — then fridge it within an hour, in a closed container, for up to 3 days. The reason for hurrying is the same reason all cooked rice is treated carefully: Bacillus cereus survives cooking as a spore and multiplies in rice left sitting at room temperature, and it produces a toxin that reheating does not destroy. Warm rice left out for an afternoon in a Pretoria summer is genuinely the riskiest thing in this recipe, and coconut fat makes it no safer. Reheat it properly hot, either steamed for 5 minutes or with a splash of water in a covered pan, and the texture comes back well; the coconut fat sets in the fridge and re-melts, so cold nasi uduk looks unpromising and reheats beautifully. It freezes for a month in portion bags. Do not reheat it twice.","chefNotes":"Rinse until the water is cloudy, not clear — a little surface starch is wanted here. Dilute the coconut milk with water, roughly half and half, because undiluted coconut milk makes greasy rice with hard centres. Then two stages, always: absorb in the coconut milk on the lowest heat until the liquid is gone and the surface craters, then finish in steam with the lid on so nothing is boiling against hot metal. Ten minutes standing, then fluff with a fork, never a spoon.","pairsWith":"Nasi uduk is a base and it is built on. The classic Jakarta plate is coconut rice with fried chicken or fried tempe, a spoonful of sambal, a scattering of fried shallots, some sliced cucumber and half a boiled egg — pieces added and left off freely. It is also the correct rice under rendang, since the coconut in both makes them read as one dish rather than two, and it is what turns a plate of gado-gado from a big lunch into a full supper.","trivia":"Nasi uduk is Betawi food, and Betawi is the most interesting answer to the question of what Jakarta's own culture is. The Betawi are the people of the old city itself, and they are a creole formed over roughly three centuries in and around the port: Malay, Sundanese and Javanese, Balinese and Bugis, Chinese traders long settled, Arab and Indian merchants, Portuguese-speaking freed slaves from across the Indian Ocean, and the Dutch administration all in one crowded delta town. The food came out of that mixture rather than out of any single tradition, which is why Betawi cooking pairs a Malay coconut technique with Chinese fried shallots and a Portuguese-descended taste for things stewed with tomato. The name itself carries the history: Betawi is simply the local rendering of Batavia, the name the Dutch East India Company gave the town it built on the ruins of Jayakarta in 1619 — so the people of Jakarta are, in their own language, named after a foreign fort, and the food they made in its shadow belongs to none of the traditions that fort was trading with.\n\nIt is also, properly, a breakfast. Nasi uduk is cart food sold before dawn, spooned onto a banana leaf with whatever the seller has, wrapped into a cone and eaten standing up on the way somewhere — the fried chicken and the sambal and the egg are all optional extras negotiated at the cart, which is why no two descriptions of the dish agree on what is in it. And it has a well-known twin across the water: nasi lemak, the Malaysian and Singaporean national breakfast, is the same coconut rice by another name, and the two countries have a low-grade running argument about ownership that neither can win, since the technique is older than either border. The one real difference is that nasi lemak is usually tied to a fixed set of accompaniments while nasi uduk is not. The third version below, nasi kuning, is the same rice turned yellow with turmeric, and yellow is doing symbolic work there rather than culinary — gold is the colour of prosperity and thanksgiving, which is why nasi kuning is moulded into a cone called a tumpeng, mountain-shaped, and cut at birthdays, weddings and office anniversaries all over the country.","howThisFeels":"Lifting the lid after twenty-five minutes of not lifting the lid, and the whole kitchen going warm and sweet with coconut in about two seconds.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold nasi uduk fried in a hot pan with a little oil until the bottom catches into a crust — the coconut fat already in it browns better than plain rice ever does.","(easy) Pressed into a small bowl, turned out onto a plate and topped with a fried egg and sambal, which is a complete meal in four minutes.","(medium) Formed into balls around a spoonful of leftover rendang or fried tempe, rolled in a little flour and shallow-fried into crisp rice cakes.","(creative) Left uncovered in the fridge overnight so it dries properly, then crumbled and deep-fried in small clumps as a crunchy topping for soup or salad — the coconut in it makes these far better than plain fried rice crumbs."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Nasi Uduk Sederhana — Coconut and Salt Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":12,"howThisFeels":"Three things in a pot and it still fills the kitchen with the smell that matters.","trivia":"This is what most households actually cook, and the reason is arithmetic rather than laziness: galangal and lemongrass together cost several times more than the rice they are flavouring, and they are bought by the piece rather than the gram. Dropping them costs the dish its high aromatic top note and takes nothing at all off the coconut, which is the flavour anyone would name if asked what nasi uduk tastes like. The two-stage method is unchanged and is doing all the important work — this version is proof that the technique, not the aromatics, is what makes coconut rice good.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"8g galangal"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"12g lemongrass","to":"6g lemongrass"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Leave the galangal out and halve the lemongrass to 6g, bruised as described. Everything else is identical — the dilution, the absorption stage, the steam, the ten minutes standing, the fork. If you have no lemongrass at all, this still works and is still nasi uduk; add a strip of lime zest to the pot instead, removed with the bay at the end, for a little of the citrus lift the lemongrass was providing."}]}},{"name":"Nasi Uduk Betawi — the Full Aromatic Pot","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"Grains that fall apart separately off the fork, each one carrying coconut, lemongrass and something faintly piney underneath.","trivia":"The aromatics in a Betawi pot are chosen to sit under the coconut rather than beside it, and galangal is the one that does the real work. It is not a hotter ginger, whatever it looks like — the compounds are different, and it reads as resinous and faintly medicinal rather than sharp, which is exactly the note that stops a pot of coconut rice tasting flatly sweet. In Jakarta a pandan leaf usually goes in as well, knotted, for a grassy vanilla smell that is hard to describe and instantly recognisable; it is sold frozen in South Africa in Asian grocers and is worth adding if you find it, one knotted leaf, pulled out at the end with the rest."},{"name":"Nasi Kuning — the Yellow Celebration Rice (Vegan)","icon":"🌾","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"A pot of gold-coloured rice that makes an ordinary Tuesday supper look like somebody remembered an occasion.","trivia":"Nasi kuning is nasi uduk plus turmeric, and it exists for a reason that has nothing to do with flavour: yellow is the colour of gold, prosperity and thanksgiving across much of the archipelago, so the rice is what you serve when there is something to mark. It is packed into a cone-shaped mould and turned out as a tumpeng, a mountain of yellow rice ringed with side dishes, and the tip of the cone is cut and given to the guest of honour — at weddings, at birthdays, at the opening of a shop. Turmeric also does something quietly practical: it is mildly antimicrobial and it was a preservative long before it was a decoration, which is part of why yellow rice travelled to feasts in the heat.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"4g ground turmeric"},{"item":"10ml lime juice"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add the 4g of ground turmeric to the pot at the very start, whisked into the coconut milk and water before the rice goes in, so the colour is even — sprinkled on top of dry rice it streaks and never distributes. If you have fresh turmeric, use about 15g grated and squeezed, which gives a cleaner colour and less of the dusty note ground turmeric can leave. Stir the 10ml of lime juice in with the liquid too: the acid brightens the yellow noticeably, since curcumin shifts colour with pH, and it lifts what would otherwise be a slightly earthy pot. Cook it in exactly the same two stages. To serve it properly, pack the hot rice firmly into a bowl or a cone of rolled banana leaf, leave it two minutes, and turn it out — it holds its shape while hot and collapses once cool, so mould it at the last moment."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥘"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-nasi-goreng","name":"Nasi Goreng","nameAlt":"Nasi Goreng — yesterday's rice, dried out in the fridge overnight, fried one portion at a time in a pan hot enough that it never gets the chance to steam","aliases":["Nasi Goreng","Indonesian Fried Rice","Nasi Goreng Ayam","Nasi Goreng Kampung","Nasi Goreng Pattaya","Fried Rice Indonesian"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","quick","leftovers","street-food","breakfast","budget"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","breakfast","lunch","quick"],"ingredients":"120g chicken thighs · 80g white rice · 40g cucumber · 30g shallots · 20ml kecap manis · 20ml sunflower oil · 15g red chilli · 10g garlic · 10g spring onions · 5g shrimp paste · 1 large egg","method":"This dish is decided the day before, and no amount of skill on the day will get around it. COOK THE RICE A DAY AHEAD. The 80g of white rice, cooked plain and drained, gives you roughly 200g cooked — spread it on a tray while it is still hot so it cools fast, then refrigerate it uncovered or loosely covered overnight. What happens in those hours is the whole reason nasi goreng exists as a distinct dish: the surface of every grain dries and firms, and the starch retrogrades and stops being sticky. Freshly cooked rice is soft and holds a great deal of water at its surface, and dropped into a hot pan it releases that water as steam and then absorbs the sauce like a sponge. You get a soft, pale, faintly gluey mound. Cold day-old rice fries. It separates into individual grains, takes colour, and stays chewy. If you must use rice cooked today, spread it thin on a tray in front of a fan for an hour and accept that it will be second best.\n\nBreak the cold rice up with your fingers before anything else, in a bowl, until there is not a single clump left. Cold rice comes out of the fridge in a solid block, and clumps that go into the pan intact will still be clumps on the plate — the outside of a lump takes all the sauce and colour and the inside stays white and bland. This takes two minutes and it is worth doing properly.\n\nNow make the bumbu. Pound or blitz the 30g of shallots, the 15g of red chilli, the 10g of garlic and the 5g of shrimp paste into a rough paste. Toast the terasi first if you have not already — wrapped in a little foil, dry pan, about 2 minutes a side — because raw shrimp paste stays harsh right through a fast fry and this is a fast fry. Leave the paste coarse rather than smooth; you want it to fry in flecks rather than smear into a film.\n\nCut the 120g of chicken thighs into pieces about 2cm, slice the 10g of spring onions, keeping the white and green apart, and cut the 40g of cucumber into thick slices for the side of the plate. Have the 20ml of kecap manis measured out in a spoon within reach. From the moment the pan gets hot this dish takes about five minutes and there is no time to look for anything.\n\nHERE IS THE RULE THAT DECIDES WHETHER THIS WORKS AT HOME, and it is the one nobody puts in a recipe: COOK ONE PORTION AT A TIME. A domestic hob delivers a small fraction of the heat a street cart's burner does, and the pan is the only thing holding that heat. Put two portions of cold rice into a home wok and the temperature crashes below the point where water flashes off on contact — so the rice sits in its own steam, goes soft, and comes out grey. One portion in a wide pan, over the highest heat you have, and the pan recovers between additions. If you are feeding four, fry four times, five minutes each, and keep the finished plates warm. That is genuinely how it is done at a warung, one plate to an order, and it is why warung nasi goreng tastes better than anything made in a big batch at home.\n\nGet the pan properly hot before the oil goes in — a drop of water flicked in should skitter and vanish rather than sit and bubble. Add the 20ml of sunflower oil, swirl it, and put in the chicken in a single layer. Leave it undisturbed for a full minute so it takes real colour, then stir it about for another 2 minutes until cooked through. Push it to one side or lift it out.\n\nAdd the bumbu to the hot oil and fry it, stirring, for 60 to 90 seconds. Two things tell you it is done: the smell changes from raw onion and sharp shrimp into something rounded and savoury, and the paste stops looking wet. This second cue matters more than it sounds — a wet paste puts water into a pan that is about to receive rice, and the rice steams. You are frying the water out of the bumbu, not merely warming it.\n\nNow the rice, all at once, and turn up whatever heat is left. Toss and press it against the hot base with the back of a spatula, then lift and turn, then press again — pressing puts grains in direct contact with hot metal, which is where the toasted, faintly nutty flavour comes from, and lifting stops them burning. Keep it moving for 2 to 3 minutes until the rice is hot through, glossy with oil and beginning to catch in places. Add the chicken back and the white of the spring onion.\n\nTHEN THE KECAP MANIS, AND POUR IT DOWN THE SIDE OF THE PAN, NOT ONTO THE RICE. This is the last technical point and the most commonly wrecked. Kecap manis is roughly a third sugar; dropped into the middle of a pile of rice it soaks straight into whichever grains it lands on, so you get a few dark salty-sweet clumps and a lot of plain rice. Poured around the hot edge it hits bare metal, caramelises for a second or two, and then you fold it through as a thin hot syrup that coats everything evenly. The other half of the same rule is that it goes in at the END. Add it early and you have ten minutes of sugar over high heat, which is not caramelising, it is burning — bitter, black flecks, and a dish that tastes scorched rather than smoky. Toss hard for about 30 seconds until every grain has gone an even mahogany, then take it off the heat and stir in the green of the spring onion.\n\nFry the egg separately, in a hot pan with a little oil, hard and fast so the white blisters and the edges go brown and frilly while the yolk stays runny. Slide it on top of the rice, put the cucumber on the side, and eat it immediately. The runny yolk broken into the rice at the table is doing a real job — nasi goreng is dry by design and the yolk is the sauce.\n\nOne honest note on the shortcut, since it is sold in every supermarket: a jar of ready-made nasi goreng paste will get you a decent plate of food and skips the pounding. What it cannot do is give you the toasted terasi note or let you set the chilli where you want it, and it is dearer per portion than the shallots and garlic it replaces. Use it on a weeknight without guilt; do not expect it to taste like this.","cookTime":"Rice a day ahead · Prep 15 min · Fry 5 min per portion","kcal":"~590 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 33g, Carbs 62g, Fat 23g, Sodium ~1080mg","storage":"This section matters more here than in most recipes, and not because of the fried rice — because of the plain rice that goes into it. Cooked rice is the classic vehicle for Bacillus cereus, a bacterium whose spores survive boiling comfortably. Leave cooked rice standing at room temperature and those spores germinate and the cells produce a toxin, and the important part is this: THAT TOXIN IS HEAT-STABLE AND FRYING DOES NOT DESTROY IT. Reheating unsafe rice does not make it safe. So the overnight rice this dish depends on must be cooled fast — spread on a tray, not left in the pot — and into the fridge within an hour, used within a day, and reheated only once. Done that way it is completely safe and always has been; the problem is rice left out overnight on a counter, which is a different thing entirely. The finished nasi goreng is itself a reheated rice dish, which is why it should be eaten straight away rather than kept: fry it, eat it, and do not put it back in the fridge for tomorrow. If you have made too much, that is the one time to break the rule — cool it fast, fridge it, eat it within a day and reheat it properly hot once. Do not freeze it; the rice goes chalky and the egg is ruined. Uncooked cold plain rice, on the other hand, is the whole point and holds its day in the fridge perfectly.","chefNotes":"Cook the rice the day before and chill it uncovered — this is not a shortcut you can skip, it is the dish. Break every clump with your fingers before it goes near the pan. Fry the bumbu until it stops looking wet, because a wet paste steams the rice. ONE PORTION AT A TIME on a home hob. And the kecap manis goes in LAST, poured down the hot side of the pan so it caramelises rather than soaking into a few grains.","pairsWith":"It is built to be eaten with things scattered on and around it rather than with a side dish: a fried egg on top is close to compulsory, prawn crackers for scooping, a spoonful of sambal on the rim, sliced cucumber and tomato for something cold and raw against the heat. Fried tempe or a few sticks of satay alongside turn it from a plate into a meal. Sweet hot tea is the traditional drink and it works better than it sounds against the salt.","trivia":"Fried rice dishes cluster in hot, rice-eating parts of the world, and the reason is older and more practical than any recipe: before refrigeration, cooked rice left overnight in a tropical climate was genuinely dangerous, and the safest thing to do with it in the morning was to cook it again, hard and fast, with salt and spice and whatever protein was to hand. Frying yesterday's rice was food management before it was a cuisine. The modern kitchen has changed the reasoning without changing the dish — we now know that the culprit, Bacillus cereus, produces a toxin that a hot pan will not destroy, so the fridge does the work that morning frying used to attempt, and the frying is now purely for pleasure.\n\nWhat makes nasi goreng specifically Indonesian rather than simply fried rice is a single bottle. Chinese traders brought the technique of frying cooked rice to the archipelago centuries ago, and it landed in a place that already had a thick sweet soy of its own. Kecap manis is a genuinely local invention rather than an import: soy sauce arrived from China, and Javanese producers reworked it with large quantities of palm sugar into something closer to a syrup, dark, viscous and only mildly salty. That single substitution — sweet soy where a Chinese cook would use light soy and a Thai cook fish sauce — is what turns fried rice mahogany instead of pale, and it is why the dish tastes sweet, savoury and faintly smoky all at once rather than simply savoury. Indonesia's national dish is, in effect, a Chinese method rewritten by an Indonesian condiment.\n\nThe rest of it is ordinary domestic life, which is the best argument for it. Nasi goreng is overwhelmingly a breakfast, eaten early and often standing up, and it is the single most common thing an Indonesian household makes without thinking about it. That is also why nobody can tell you the recipe: it takes whatever is in the kitchen, and the versions with names — kampung with dried anchovies, pattaya wrapped in an omelette blanket, jawa with a heavier hand on the chilli paste — are just households that agreed with each other for long enough.","howThisFeels":"The hiss the second cold rice hits a properly hot pan, and then the smell about four minutes later when the sweet soy goes down the side and catches for a moment before you fold it through.","leftovers":["(easy) Fry it a second time in a hot pan and press it flat into a cake, left alone until the underside forms a crust, then slid out and cut in wedges — better than reheated fried rice has any right to be.","(easy) Packed cold into a lunch box with extra cucumber and a spoonful of sambal, which is how half of Jakarta eats lunch.","(medium) Stirred into a beaten egg with a little flour and fried as thick fritters, which is a real Indonesian use for the last of the rice and turns a small amount into a plateful.","(creative) Pressed firmly into a small bowl, turned out, and pan-fried on both faces until crusted, then topped with a fresh fried egg — a two-minute rebuild that eats like a new dish rather than a leftover."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Nasi Goreng Sederhana — Egg Only (Budget · Vegetarian)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"Five minutes, one pan, one egg, and the smell that says somebody is looking after you.","trivia":"This is the honest everyday version and the one that shows what the dish actually is. Strip out the chicken and the shrimp paste and nothing structural is lost, because the flavour was never coming from the protein — it comes from the bumbu, the kecap manis and the heat of the pan. In Indonesia this is what gets made at eleven at night and at six in the morning, and the fried egg on top is not a garnish but the entire protein content of the meal. It is also the version to learn on: with fewer things in the pan the temperature holds better, so the rice fries harder and you can see clearly what a properly fried grain looks like.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"120g chicken thighs"},{"item":"5g shrimp paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the chicken and the terasi and go straight from the bumbu to the rice. Without the shrimp paste the bumbu loses its savoury bottom note, so compensate deliberately rather than pretending it is the same: add an extra 5ml of kecap manis and, if you have it, a small pinch of salt with the shallots, and fry the paste a full 90 seconds so the shallots themselves brown and go sweet. With less in the pan everything moves faster, so have the kecap manis in the spoon before you start. Two eggs instead of one is the usual move here, one fried on top and one scrambled through the rice — push the rice to one side of the pan, pour a beaten egg into the space, let it set for twenty seconds and then break it up and fold it through."}]}},{"name":"Nasi Goreng Ayam — with Chicken","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":35,"howThisFeels":"One plate at a time, five minutes each, and standing at the stove eating the first one while the second is frying.","trivia":"Thigh rather than breast, and it is not only about flavour. A wok-fried dish gives a piece of chicken about three minutes of very high heat, and lean breast meat passes from raw through done to dry inside that window — there is no margin. Thigh carries enough fat and connective tissue to stay tender well past the point where it is cooked, so it forgives the extra thirty seconds you will inevitably spend on the rice. The same logic explains why street cooks so often use chicken cut small rather than in chunks: more surface for the same weight means it colours properly in the time available."},{"name":"Nasi Goreng Sayur — with Tempe (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":50,"howThisFeels":"Chewier and nuttier than the original, and the tempe holds the sweet soy better than chicken ever does.","trivia":"Tempe is the natural protein for a vegan nasi goreng rather than a compromise, and the reason is porosity. Chicken is coated by a sauce; tempe drinks it, because it is thousands of whole beans held in a mesh with air between them, so the kecap manis goes into the pieces rather than sitting on them. It also browns hard and holds its shape in a pan that is being tossed constantly. The one thing that has to be handled is the terasi: dropping the shrimp paste takes out the savoury depth that made the bumbu taste like more than fried onions, and the honest replacement is not a substitute ingredient but technique — brown the shallots properly and let the tempe itself take real colour, because both are doing the job the fermented paste was doing.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"120g chicken thighs"},{"item":"5g shrimp paste"},{"item":"1 large egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"100g tempeh"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the tempe first and fry it properly. Cut 100g of tempeh into 1cm cubes, dry the faces, and fry them in the oil over medium-high heat for 5 to 6 minutes until at least two faces of each cube are deep gold and firm — do not rush this, because pale tempe in a fried rice is bland and slightly chalky while browned tempe is the best thing in the bowl. Lift it out and set it aside, then fry the bumbu in the same oil. With no terasi and no egg the dish needs its savoury weight from browning, so give the bumbu a full 90 seconds and let the shallots colour. Add the rice, then the tempe back, then the kecap manis down the side of the pan as described. Finish with the spring onion greens and, if you want the richness the egg was providing, a spoonful of sambal and a scatter of fried shallots on top."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-perkedel","name":"Perkedel","nameAlt":"Perkedel — the potato is FRIED before it is mashed, never boiled, which is the whole reason these hold together without a spoonful of flour","aliases":["Perkedel","Perkedel Kentang","Bergedel","Indonesian Potato Fritters","Perkedel Jagung","Frikadel","Potato Patties"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","side","snack","fried","potato","budget","party"],"diet":["omnivore","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","party","lunchbox","guests"],"ingredients":"150g potato · 60g beef mince · 40ml sunflower oil · 20g shallots · 10g celery · 5g garlic · 3g salt · 2g white pepper · 2g ground nutmeg · 1 large egg","method":"Everything about this recipe hangs on one instruction, and it is the one every home cook gets wrong: DO NOT BOIL THE POTATO. Peel the 150g of potato, cut it into slices about 1cm thick, and shallow-fry the slices in the 40ml of sunflower oil over medium heat for 6 to 8 minutes, turning once, until they are tender right through and pale gold at the edges. Then lift them out and mash them.\n\nHere is why, because it is the difference between a fritter and a disaster. A boiled potato has absorbed water — it went into water and it came out of water, and that water is trapped in the starch. Mash it and you get a wet purée, and a wet purée in hot oil does two things: it spits, and it slumps, because there is nothing holding it together but starch that is already saturated. Every recipe that boils the potato then has to correct for it, which is why so many perkedel recipes quietly add flour or breadcrumbs. Frying takes moisture OUT instead of putting it in. The slices come out drier than they went in, the mash is stiff and slightly crumbly, and it binds on its own with nothing but an egg. No flour, no crumbs, no cornflour. If you find yourself reaching for a thickener, the potato was boiled.\n\nMash while the slices are still hot, with a fork or a masher and never a food processor or a stick blender. Hot potato mashes into fluff; cold potato mashes into glue. And a machine ruptures the starch granules and whips the released starch into a stretchy, gummy paste — the same failure as overworked mashed potato, and once it happens there is no recovering it. Mash until it is mostly smooth with a few small lumps left, then STOP.\n\nWhile it cools, fry the mince. Finely chop the 20g of shallots, the 5g of garlic and the 10g of celery. In whatever oil is left in the pan, fry the shallots and garlic for 2 minutes until soft and fragrant, add the 60g of beef mince, and cook it for 5 to 6 minutes, breaking it up with a spoon, until it is browned and — this is the important part — properly DRY. Keep it on the heat until the liquid in the pan has gone and the mince starts to sizzle rather than steam. Wet mince put into dry mash undoes the work you just did with the potato. Let it cool a little.\n\nThe celery goes in raw at the end rather than being cooked with the mince, and it is not filler. Indonesian perkedel almost always carries either celery leaf or spring onion, and what it provides is a green, slightly bitter freshness cutting through what is otherwise potato, meat and fat. Left raw it keeps that; cooked with the mince it disappears.\n\nMix the mash, the mince mixture, the raw celery, the 3g of salt, the 2g of white pepper and the 2g of ground nutmeg together with your hands. Nutmeg is the signature and it is worth being precise about the quantity: 2g is roughly half a teaspoon of freshly grated, and it is doing the same job it does in a Dutch meatball, which is exactly where this dish gets it from. Less and the fritter tastes of nothing in particular. More and it takes over completely and starts to taste of milk pudding. White pepper rather than black, because black pepper's fruity note fights the nutmeg and the specks look like burnt bits.\n\nNow the egg, and use it as a COATING rather than mixing it in, which is the traditional method and a better one. Beat the 1 large egg in a shallow bowl. Shape the potato mixture into small flattened patties about 5cm across and 1.5cm thick, pressing them firmly between your palms so there are no cracks — a crack is where a fritter breaks apart in the oil. Then CHILL THEM for at least 20 minutes, uncovered, in the fridge. This is not optional and it is not resting for its own sake: cold patties are firm patties, and firm patties survive the two seconds between the egg bowl and the oil. Warm ones sag off the spoon.\n\nEgg mixed through the mixture works, and is how many households do it, but it makes the inside softer and slightly custardy and it makes the fritters harder to shape. Dipping instead gives you a thin sealed skin of set egg on the outside, which browns beautifully, holds the shape absolutely, and keeps the inside light rather than dense. If you would rather mix it in, use only half the egg or the mixture will be too slack to shape.\n\nHeat oil in a frying pan to a moderate 170°C — enough to come halfway up the patties, so about 1cm. Moderate, not hot: these are already cooked through, so all the heat is doing is browning the outside and setting the egg, and fierce oil browns the skin before the inside has warmed. Dip each chilled patty into the beaten egg, let the excess run off for a second, and lower it in. Fry 2 to 3 minutes a side, in a single layer with room between them, turning ONCE. Turning repeatedly is the other way people lose them; each turn is a chance for a fritter to break, and the crust needs an uninterrupted couple of minutes to set hard enough to be handled.\n\nThey are done when they are deep even gold and firm enough that you can pick one up by its edge. Drain on a rack, not on paper — paper traps steam under the bottom face and turns the crust you just built back to soft. Salt lightly and eat them warm.","cookTime":"Fry potato 8 min · Mix 10 min · Chill 20 min · Fry 6 min","kcal":"~340 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 17g, Carbs 26g, Fat 19g, Sodium ~780mg","storage":"The raw mixture is the part that keeps well and the fried fritters are the part that does not, so work backwards from when you want to eat. Shaped uncooked patties hold 2 days in the fridge on a covered plate in a single layer, and they are actually better on day two because the mixture firms up further and the nutmeg spreads through it. They also freeze raw very well for 2 months — freeze them apart on a tray first, then bag them, and fry from frozen at a slightly lower temperature for a minute or two longer. Fried perkedel keeps 3 days in the fridge but goes soft, and the fix is dry heat rather than a microwave: 6 to 8 minutes in a hot oven or 4 minutes in an air fryer brings the crust back almost completely, while a microwave turns them to damp mash. Do not freeze them cooked; the potato goes grainy and the egg skin weeps. And do not leave them stacked or covered while warm — trapped steam is the enemy of everything you fried them for.","chefNotes":"Fry the potato slices, never boil them — boiled potato is wet and wet mash needs flour to survive the oil, which is the compromise this method avoids entirely. Mash hot, by hand, and stop while a few lumps remain. Cook the mince properly dry. Nutmeg at 2g, white pepper not black, celery raw at the end. Then dip in beaten egg rather than mixing it through, chill 20 minutes, and turn ONCE in moderate oil.","pairsWith":"Perkedel is not really a starter in Indonesia so much as a thing that sits on the side of a plate, and its natural home is next to a bowl of soup — soto ayam above all, where one is broken into the broth and eaten half-dissolved. It belongs on any rice plate alongside sambal, and it is very good with nothing but sambal and a cold drink. A squeeze of lime over hot fritters lifts them noticeably. They are also one of the better things to hand round at a party, because they are just as good warm as hot.","trivia":"The word is Dutch and the dish is an inversion of the Dutch original, which is the interesting part. Perkedel comes from frikadel, a Dutch meatball that arrived in the archipelago during the colonial period and which itself traces back through French and Italian kitchens. The Dutch frikadel is meat, with a little bread or potato in it to stretch it and hold it together. What came out of Indonesian kitchens is the exact reverse: potato, with a little meat in it for flavour. That flip is not an accident of taste but of arithmetic — meat was expensive and potatoes were not, so the filler became the dish and the dish became the seasoning. Once you see it, you can see the same inversion running through a great deal of colonial food everywhere: the expensive thing becomes a flavouring for the cheap thing, and the result outlives the original as a dish people actually want.\n\nThe nutmeg is the fingerprint left behind. It is in perkedel because it is in a Dutch meatball, and it is in a Dutch meatball because the Netherlands spent the seventeenth century fighting a war over the Banda Islands to control the world's nutmeg — islands which are, of course, Indonesian. So the spice travelled from Indonesia to Europe as the most valuable cargo on earth, became standard in Dutch home cooking, and then came back to its own islands inside a recipe for meatballs, where it stayed. Meanwhile the word kept moving in the other direction: a frikandel in the Netherlands today is not a meatball at all but a skinless deep-fried sausage sold at every snack bar, a completely different food wearing the same name. One word, three foods, two continents, and the only one still made the old way is the Indonesian one.","howThisFeels":"Picking one up by the edge to check it is firm enough, and the small dry crack of the egg crust giving way to something that is almost fluffy inside.","leftovers":["(easy) Broken into a bowl of hot chicken broth and left thirty seconds to half-dissolve, which is exactly how they are eaten with soto and is arguably better than eating them whole.","(easy) Split and pressed into a bread roll with sambal and sliced cucumber — a genuinely good sandwich and a standard Indonesian street filling.","(medium) Crumbled and fried again in a hot dry pan until crisp, then scattered over rice or a salad as a savoury crumb, which uses up even the broken ones.","(creative) Flattened thinner, topped with a fried egg and a spoonful of sambal, and eaten as a breakfast rösti — the nutmeg makes far more sense at breakfast than it has any right to."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Perkedel Kentang — Potato Only (Budget · Vegetarian)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":14,"howThisFeels":"Softer, sweeter and more about the nutmeg — the version that tastes most like the inside of a good pie.","trivia":"Without the mince this becomes the plainest and most common form of the dish, and it is worth knowing that the meatless version is not the concession — in most of Indonesia it is the default, and the meat one is what appears when there is meat. It also shows how much work the aromatics are doing: with the beef gone, the shallots, garlic, celery and nutmeg are the entire flavour, so they get slightly increased rather than left as they were. This is the version that goes with soto, where the broth supplies the meatiness the fritter is not carrying.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"60g beef mince"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"20g shallots","to":"30g shallots"},{"from":"10g celery","to":"15g celery"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Leave the mince out and increase the aromatics deliberately, because with the beef gone they are all the flavour there is: 30g of shallots and 15g of celery instead of 20g and 10g. Fry the shallots and garlic as described and let them go properly golden rather than merely soft, 4 to 5 minutes — browned shallot is doing the savoury job the mince was doing. Everything else is unchanged, including the fried potato, the hot mash and the 20-minute chill. Without the mince the mixture is a little softer, so press the patties slightly thicker and give them the full 20 minutes cold, and consider adding a second raw green — spring onion greens alongside the celery work well."}]}},{"name":"Perkedel Kentang Daging — with Beef","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":21,"howThisFeels":"The nutmeg hits first and the beef arrives second, which is not the order you expect from something that looks like a potato cake.","trivia":"Sixty grams of mince to a hundred and fifty of potato is a deliberate ratio and not a mean one. Push the meat much higher and the fritter stops binding — mince contributes no starch, so beyond about a third by weight there is not enough potato left to hold the thing together and you are back to needing flour. It is the same structural limit that governs a fishcake or a croquette. The other reason to keep it low is that the mince must be cooked dry before it goes in, and dry mince is quite intense; at this ratio it reads as a savoury seasoning running through the potato, which is what perkedel is supposed to taste like."},{"name":"Perkedel Jagung — Sweetcorn Fritters","icon":"🌽","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":21,"howThisFeels":"Crisp, ragged edges and whole corn kernels bursting sweet against the salt — a completely different thing wearing the same name.","trivia":"Perkedel jagung is arguably the more popular of the two across Indonesia, and structurally it is not a potato fritter at all. There is no mash and no binding problem to solve, because whole corn kernels sit in a thin spiced batter and the fritter is deliberately loose and lacy with kernels sticking out of the edges. That raggedness is the point: every protruding kernel goes crisp and slightly caramelised in the oil in a way a smooth patty never can. The technique that matters is crushing about a third of the corn before mixing — the crushed kernels release starch and milky liquid which binds the rest, so the batter needs almost no flour, which is the same principle as the fried potato in the main version arriving from a different direction.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"150g potato"},{"item":"60g beef mince"}],"addIng":[{"item":"180g sweetcorn"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No potato and no mash. Take the 180g of sweetcorn, and crush roughly a third of it — in a mortar, or by pulsing briefly in a processor, or simply by pressing it hard with the back of a fork on a board. You want it broken and milky, not puréed. That released starch is what binds the fritter, so do not skip it and do not crush the lot or you lose the burst of whole kernels that makes the dish. Mix the crushed and whole corn with the finely chopped shallots, garlic and raw celery, the salt, the white pepper, the nutmeg and the whole beaten egg — the egg goes IN here rather than being used as a dip, because there is no patty to coat. The mixture should just hold together in a spoon; if it runs, add a teaspoon of rice flour and no more. No chilling needed. Drop heaped spoonfuls into 170°C oil, flatten each one slightly with the back of the spoon so the edges spread out ragged, and fry 2 to 3 minutes a side until deep gold. Do not tidy the edges — the loose kernels around the rim are the best part."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Nasi Goreng","target":"indonesia-nasi-goreng","emoji":"🍛"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-sate-ayam","name":"Sate Ayam","nameAlt":"Sate Ayam — thin flat slices cut across the grain, threaded tight, and cooked over two heats rather than one, because the fire is the recipe","aliases":["Sate Ayam","Satay Ayam","Chicken Satay","Sate","Satay","Sate Tempe","Chicken Skewers Indonesian"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","main","grilled","braai","skewer","street-food","party","chicken"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["braai","party","family","guests","street-food","everyday"],"ingredients":"160g chicken thighs · 30g raw peanuts · 20ml kecap manis · 20ml sunflower oil · 15g shallots · 10g garlic · 10g red chilli · 10g jaggery · 10ml lime juice · 5g coriander seeds · 5g tamarind paste · 3g salt · 4 bamboo skewers","method":"SOAK THE SKEWERS FIRST, before you touch the chicken. Put the 4 bamboo skewers in cold water for at least 30 minutes and leave them there until the moment you thread them. Dry bamboo over coals is kindling — it chars through at the thin end and a skewer snaps in half while you are turning it, usually into the fire. Soaked bamboo has water in the fibres that has to boil off before the wood can burn, which buys you the eight or nine minutes this takes. It is thirty seconds of work and it is the difference between four skewers and three.\n\nNow the cut, which is the first half of what makes sate sate rather than kebabs. Take the 160g of chicken thighs, boned, and lay each one flat. Find the direction the muscle fibres run, then slice ACROSS them into thin flat pieces about 5mm thick and 4cm long — flat slices, not cubes. Two things follow from this. Across the grain shortens the fibres so each piece is tender rather than stringy, which matters far more on a fast fierce cook than in a slow braise. And flat is a heat calculation: a 5mm slice is cooked through in about the same time its surface takes to char, while a 2cm cube needs three or four times as long in the middle and will be dry and grey on the outside before the centre is safe. Every disappointing homemade satay is a cube problem.\n\nMake the marinade and the sauce from one base, because they are the same thing at different dilutions. Pound or blitz the 15g of shallots, the 10g of garlic, the 10g of red chilli, the 5g of coriander seeds and the 3g of salt into a rough paste. Take about a third of it, mix it with 10ml of the kecap manis and 10ml of the sunflower oil, and turn the chicken through it. Marinate 30 minutes at room temperature, or up to 4 hours in the fridge, and no longer — there is acid coming later but the salt alone will start to cure the surface of thin slices past a few hours and the texture goes faintly hammy.\n\nFor the sauce, fry the 30g of raw peanuts in the remaining 10ml of oil over medium heat for 4 to 5 minutes, stirring constantly, until they are a shade darker and smell strongly toasted, then lift them out to cool and crisp. Watch them the whole time; peanuts go from perfect to acrid in about twenty seconds and there is no rescuing a burnt batch. Pound the cooled peanuts with the remaining two-thirds of the paste, the 10g of jaggery and the 5g of tamarind paste, then loosen with hot water a spoonful at a time to a thick pouring consistency, and finish with the 10ml of lime juice off the heat. Keep it coarse.\n\nNow thread, and thread TIGHTLY. Push the slices onto the soaked skewers so each piece is pressed flat against the next with no gaps, filling only the top two-thirds and leaving the bottom third bare as a handle. Gaps are not a cosmetic issue: an air gap lets the fire get at bare wood, and it also lets the two exposed faces of each slice dry out and go leathery. Pressed together, the meat behaves like one continuous strip and cooks evenly. Four skewers from 160g means roughly 40g each, which is about four or five slices — resist loading them heavier, because a fat skewer cannot cook fast, and fast is the entire point.\n\nHERE IS THE FIRE, AND IT IS THE RECIPE. Build or bank your coals so there are TWO ZONES: one area with the coals raked into a deep hot bed, and one area with a thin scattering or none at all. If you are on a gas grill, one burner high and one low or off. Almost everyone cooks satay over a single uniform fierce fire, and it is why the skewers come off with black stripes and raw pink middles, or dry through with no colour at all — a single heat forces you to choose. Two zones let you do both jobs separately.\n\nStart over the HOT zone and leave the skewers alone for 60 to 90 seconds a side. They should hiss immediately. You are after real colour on both faces — brown, with a few genuinely black edges — and this happens fast and needs no help. Do not brush anything on yet and do not fidget with them; meat lifted too early tears and sticks.\n\nThen move them to the COOL zone and only now start glazing. Brush with the remaining kecap manis, turn, brush the other side, turn again, for 2 to 3 minutes in total, until each skewer carries a dark sticky lacquer. Kecap manis is roughly a third sugar, so over the hot zone it would be black and bitter in well under a minute. Over the cool side it thickens, catches and sets. The reason for the order is worth stating plainly: char first over high heat, sweeten second over low. Get it the other way round and the sugar burns long before the meat has browned, and you taste scorched syrup instead of grilled chicken.\n\nThey are done when the meat is firm and springy to the touch, the glaze looks like varnish and the thin edges of the slices are crisp. Total time on the fire is about five minutes, which is why the preparation matters so much — there is no window in which to fix anything. Rest them 2 minutes off the heat, then serve with the peanut sauce in a bowl to dip into rather than poured over, and a wedge of lime.\n\nIf you have no fire at all, this works in a heavy ridged pan indoors, and the two-zone rule becomes a two-temperature rule: pan on high for the char, then turn the heat down to low before the glaze goes anywhere near it. What you lose is smoke, and there is no honest way to fake it.","cookTime":"Soak 30 min · Marinate 30 min · Sauce 12 min · Grill 5 min","kcal":"~490 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g, Carbs 21g, Fat 27g, Sodium ~940mg","storage":"Everything except the grilling holds, which makes this a genuinely good dish to prepare ahead for people. Threaded raw skewers keep 24 hours in the fridge, covered, laid flat in a single layer — and the marinade is doing its work in there, so a batch threaded in the morning is better at six than one threaded at five. Do not push past a day; the salt starts curing the thin slices and the texture turns. Raw threaded skewers also freeze for 2 months, laid apart on a tray first then bagged, and they thaw in the fridge overnight with no loss. The peanut sauce keeps 5 days in a sealed jar and thickens to a paste in the cold, which is normal — loosen it with hot water and stir hard and it comes back exactly; it freezes 3 months and an ice cube tray is the sensible way to do it. Grilled skewers keep 2 days in the fridge and are good cold, straight from it, which is how they are usually eaten the next day. Reheating is the weak point: dry heat in a hot oven for 4 minutes is passable, a microwave turns the glaze wet and the meat tough. Never leave cooked skewers sitting warm for an hour — the glaze goes tacky and the thin slices dry out from both faces at once.","chefNotes":"Soak the bamboo 30 minutes or a skewer will snap into the fire. Slice the thigh ACROSS the grain into flat 5mm pieces, never cubes. Thread tight with no gaps and leave a bare handle. Then build TWO heat zones: char hard over the hot side with nothing brushed on, move to the cool side, and only then glaze with the kecap manis, because it is a third sugar and will be bitter over high heat in under a minute. Char first, sweeten second.","pairsWith":"Sate is street food and travels with plain starch: steamed white rice, or the compressed rice cakes called lontong cut into cubes, which is the classic pairing because they soak up the peanut sauce. Raw sliced shallot and cucumber on the side is not a garnish but the counterweight — the dish is sweet, rich and smoky and it needs something cold and sharp. Sambal for anyone who wants it hotter, and prawn crackers to scoop the last of the sauce. It sits perfectly on a braai alongside anything else on the fire, which is exactly what its South African descendant does.","trivia":"South Africans already know this dish under another name, and the line between them is direct rather than coincidental. The Afrikaans sosatie comes from the Malay words for sauce and for skewered spiced meat, and it arrived at the Cape with people brought from the Indonesian archipelago by the Dutch East India Company from the middle of the seventeenth century onwards — political exiles, enslaved people, and skilled artisans, whose descendants became the Cape Malay community. They brought turmeric, tamarind, coriander seed, chilli and the whole idea of marinating meat in a spice paste before threading it onto sticks over coals. Cape Malay sosaties are, quite literally, Indonesian sate that took a boat, met local apricots and vinegar, and settled in. The braai tradition that South Africans think of as the most local thing they own has an Indonesian skewer sitting near the middle of it.\n\nThe archipelago itself did not invent skewered meat so much as make it a national institution, and the usual account is that the technique arrived with Muslim traders from India and the Middle East — kebab logic meeting Javanese spice pastes — sometime around the fifteenth to eighteenth centuries. What Indonesia added was the sauce and the sweet soy, and then multiplied it. There are dozens of regionally named sates and they are genuinely different dishes: sate lilit from Bali is minced fish and coconut moulded onto lemongrass stalks rather than skewered; sate padang from West Sumatra abandons peanuts entirely for a thick yellow turmeric-and-rice-flour gravy; sate klathak from Yogyakarta is goat threaded onto bicycle spokes, which conduct heat into the middle of the meat and cook it from the inside as well as the outside — a piece of accidental engineering no recipe can improve on. And the peanut itself is a South American plant that arrived on Portuguese and Spanish ships, which means the sauce everyone considers definitively Indonesian is younger than the skewer it is served with.","howThisFeels":"The particular hiss when eight or nine skewers hit hot coals at once, and then the change of smell a few minutes later when the sweet soy hits the cooler side and starts to caramelise.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold straight from the fridge, pulled off the stick and eaten with a spoonful of the sauce — better than reheating, and how most Indonesian households finish them.","(easy) Meat stripped off the skewers and folded through hot rice with the last of the peanut sauce loosened with a splash of water.","(medium) Chopped into a wrap or a roll with shredded cabbage, cucumber and sauce, which turns four leftover skewers into a proper lunch.","(creative) Stripped, chopped small and stirred through cold noodles with the sauce thinned to a dressing and extra lime — a cold noodle salad that is arguably better than the original the next day."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Sate Ayam Kecap — Sweet Soy Only, No Sauce (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":35,"howThisFeels":"Smokier and much sharper, with the lime and raw shallot doing all the work the peanuts usually do.","trivia":"Sate kecap is not a stripped-down version so much as the older and plainer one, and in much of Java it is what you actually get unless you ask for peanut sauce. The dip is nothing but kecap manis loosened with lime juice, with raw sliced shallot and chopped chilli dropped straight into it — no cooking at all. It works because it is doing the opposite job to a peanut sauce: instead of adding richness to lean grilled meat it adds acid and sharp raw allium, which cuts the smoke rather than padding it. It is also the honest answer when peanuts are the most expensive thing on the list, and it is what you want beside something fatty rather than beside chicken breast.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"30g raw peanuts"},{"item":"10g jaggery"},{"item":"5g tamarind paste"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"20ml kecap manis","to":"30ml kecap manis"}],"addStep":[{"text":"There is no cooked sauce at all, so the whole sauce section disappears. Use 30ml of kecap manis instead of 20ml: 10ml goes into the marinade with the oil and a third of the paste exactly as described, 10ml is brushed on over the cool zone as the glaze, and the last 10ml becomes the dip. For the dip, put that 10ml of kecap manis in a small bowl, stir in the 10ml of lime juice, and add the remaining paste RAW along with a few extra rings of finely sliced shallot and red chilli. Do not cook it and do not dilute it with water — it is meant to be sharp, thin and salty rather than creamy. Everything about the soaking, the cut, the tight threading and the two zones is unchanged, and with no rich sauce to hide behind the char matters even more, so be braver over the hot side."}]}},{"name":"Sate Ayam — with Bumbu Kacang","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":40,"howThisFeels":"Sticky dark lacquer on the outside, and then the peanut sauce arriving thick and warm over the top of the smoke.","trivia":"Thigh and not breast, and on a five-minute cook the reason is stark. Grilling gives a slice of chicken roughly two minutes a side of very high heat, and breast meat crosses from raw to done to dry inside that window with essentially no margin — there is no version of this recipe in which breast is the better choice. Thigh carries enough intramuscular fat and connective tissue that it stays juicy well past the point of being cooked, which is exactly what you need when you are also trying to blacken the outside. The fat also renders into the coals and comes back as smoke, which is a large part of why satay smells the way it does at fifty metres."},{"name":"Sate Tempe — Fermented Soy Skewers (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":74,"howThisFeels":"Chewy and nutty with hard-charred edges, and the glaze clinging to it more thickly than it ever does to chicken.","trivia":"Tempe suits a skewer better than tofu does, and the reason is structural: it is whole soya beans bound into a firm cake by a living mould, so a block can be cut into slabs that hold their shape on a stick over a fire, where tofu tears at the skewer hole and collapses. It is also porous, so it drinks the marinade all the way through in twenty minutes rather than being coated by it. The one real difference in handling is fat. Chicken thigh bastes itself as it cooks; tempe has almost none of its own, so it needs oil brushed on before and during the char or the surface dries to cardboard before it colours — and that is the whole adjustment.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"160g chicken thighs"}],"addIng":[{"item":"150g tempeh"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"20ml sunflower oil","to":"30ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cut 150g of tempeh into slabs about 1cm thick and 4cm square — noticeably thicker than the chicken slices, because tempe is already cooked and firm and thin pieces simply dry out. Thread them tight onto the soaked skewers through the flat face. Marinate 20 minutes only, which is plenty; tempe absorbs fast and a long soak makes it soggy and prone to breaking on the stick. Use 30ml of oil rather than 20ml, because tempe has no fat of its own to baste with: 15ml goes into the marinade and 15ml is brushed on during the char. Over the hot zone give it slightly longer than chicken, 2 minutes a side, and be firm about wanting real black edges — pale tempe on a skewer is bland and slightly chalky, and hard-charred tempe is superb. Then the cool zone and the kecap manis glaze exactly as described. The peanut sauce is already vegan as written, so nothing changes there."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥗"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-klepon","name":"Klepon","nameAlt":"Klepon — the palm sugar goes in SOLID and comes out LIQUID, which only works if the ball is sealed with no seam at all","aliases":["Klepon","Kelepon","Onde-Onde","Buah Melaka","Indonesian Palm Sugar Rice Balls","Pandan Rice Balls","Klepon Ubi"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","sweet","street-food","vegan","steamed","budget","party"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","party","guests","celebration"],"ingredients":"90g glutinous rice flour · 70ml water · 30g jaggery · 25g desiccated coconut · 2g salt","method":"Everything in this recipe exists to serve one moment: the ball goes into your mouth whole and then bursts with hot liquid sugar. Every instruction below is either about creating that liquid or about stopping it escaping before it reaches you. There is no other point to klepon, and a klepon that does not burst is simply a sweet dumpling.\n\nStart with the sugar, because this is where most attempts are lost. Chop the 30g of jaggery into small hard nuggets roughly 5mm across — the size of a coarse grain of rock salt. THEY MUST BE HARD AND DRY. If your jaggery is soft, damp or crumbly, spread the pieces on a plate and leave them uncovered for an hour, or give them two minutes in a low oven and let them cool completely. Here is why it matters: a hard nugget survives being wrapped in wet dough and only melts once it is inside the boiling water, which is exactly when you want it to melt. A soft or damp piece starts dissolving into the dough the moment you enclose it, and instead of a pocket of syrup you get a faintly sweet patch of dough with nothing to burst. Solid going in is what makes it liquid coming out.\n\nJaggery is standing in for gula jawa here and it is a close and honest match — both are unrefined sugars boiled down from cane or palm sap and pressed into blocks, and both carry the dark molasses depth that plain brown sugar does not. White or brown sugar will not do in this recipe, and not for flavour reasons alone: refined sugar melts to a thin colourless syrup that tastes only of sweetness, while the unrefined block melts to something dark, faintly smoky and almost savoury, which is the entire filling.\n\nPrepare the coconut before the dough, because it needs to be ready and cooling when the balls come out of the water. Toss the 25g of desiccated coconut with 1g of the salt and steam it for 5 to 8 minutes — a sieve over a pan of simmering water with a lid on works perfectly — then spread it out to cool. Do not skip this and do not use the coconut straight from the packet. Desiccated coconut is dried hard and it tastes papery and slightly stale dry, with a texture like fine sawdust; five minutes of steam rehydrates it into something soft, white, moist and sweet that actually tastes of coconut. The salt is not optional either. It is the only savoury note in the dish and without it the whole thing is flatly sweet.\n\nNow the dough. Put the 90g of glutinous rice flour in a bowl with the remaining 1g of salt and add the 70ml of water gradually — start with about 55ml, mix with a spoon and then your hand, and add the rest a teaspoon at a time. Flours vary a great deal in how much water they take depending on the mill and the day, so a fixed number is a starting point rather than an instruction. You are looking for a dough that feels like soft modelling clay: smooth, slightly tacky but not sticking to your palm, and holding a shape without cracking when you roll it. Too dry and it will split in the water and dump the sugar; too wet and you cannot seal it. Knead it two or three minutes until it is completely uniform.\n\nGlutinous rice flour is not a substitute for anything and nothing substitutes for it. It contains almost no amylose, the starch that makes ordinary rice grains stay separate, and almost entirely amylopectin, which is what gives cooked glutinous rice its chew and stretch. Plain rice flour makes a dough that boils into something short, crumbly and slightly gritty that cannot hold a liquid centre at all.\n\nNow the sealing, which is the second place people lose it. Take a piece of dough about the size of a walnut, roll it into a ball between your palms, then flatten it into a disc about 5mm thick. Put a nugget of jaggery in the middle — ONE nugget, not a heap, because the sugar liquefies and expands and an overfilled ball splits from the inside. Fold the dough up around it and pinch the edges together, then, and this is the step that matters, ROLL THE WHOLE THING BETWEEN YOUR PALMS UNTIL THE JOIN DISAPPEARS. Not until it is closed — until you cannot see where it was. Hold it up and look. A visible seam is a fault line, and in boiling water it opens, the syrup escapes into the pot, and you are left with a hollow ball and a sweet saucepan. Do all of them, keeping the finished ones under a cloth so they do not dry out.\n\nBring a wide pan of water to a bare, lazy simmer — bubbles breaking gently, not a rolling boil. A hard boil knocks soft dough balls against each other and against the base and tears them open, which is a shame after all that sealing. Lower them in gently and give them room. They will sink to the bottom and sit there.\n\nAfter 3 or 4 minutes they will rise and bob at the surface, AND FLOATING IS NOT DONE. Floating means the outside has cooked and trapped enough steam to lift the ball; the inside is still raw flour paste, and undercooked glutinous rice flour has a distinct chalky centre you cannot mistake for anything else. GIVE THEM 2 TO 3 MINUTES MORE AFTER THEY FLOAT. That extra time is also when the sugar nugget finishes melting, so it is doing double duty. You can see it happen if you look: the balls become slightly translucent and you can just make out a darker patch shifting inside.\n\nLift them straight out with a slotted spoon and roll them in the steamed coconut IMMEDIATELY, while they are dripping wet. The water on the surface is the only glue there is, and thirty seconds later it will have been absorbed and you will be sprinkling coconut onto a bare ball and watching it fall off. Do not rinse them and do not drain them dry.\n\nEat them warm, within the hour, and preferably in one bite each — that is not a suggestion but the design of the thing. Bitten in half over a plate, the syrup runs out and you have missed it. And warm matters: as they cool the melted sugar thickens and eventually sets back towards solid, so a cold klepon has a chewy sweet centre instead of a hot liquid one. Still good. Not the same dish.","cookTime":"Steam coconut 8 min · Shape 20 min · Boil 7 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 4g, Carbs 78g, Fat 9g, Sodium ~790mg","storage":"Klepon is a same-hour food and the reason is physics rather than spoilage. The liquid centre is molten sugar, and molten sugar sets as it cools, so the burst you built the whole recipe around is gone by the time they are cold — you are left with a chewy ball with a sweet middle, which is pleasant and is not what you made. On top of that, glutinous rice dough stales the way bread does, by starch retrogradation, and the fridge accelerates it badly: refrigerated klepon go hard and grainy overnight and the coconut turns sour-tasting. So keep them covered at cool room temperature and eat them the same day, within about six hours at the outside. The workaround that genuinely works is to prepare and shape the balls, uncooked, and refrigerate them on a floured tray for up to 24 hours or freeze them for 2 months — freeze them apart first, then bag them, and boil from frozen for about 2 minutes longer. That way the boiling and the coconut happen minutes before eating, which is the only stage that cannot wait. Steamed coconut on its own keeps 2 days in the fridge. Do not freeze finished klepon; the dough weeps on thawing and the syrup leaks out of the ball from the inside.","chefNotes":"The jaggery must be chopped into hard dry nuggets — damp sugar dissolves into the dough and there is nothing left to burst. Steam the desiccated coconut with salt first or it tastes like sawdust. Seal each ball by rolling it between your palms until the join is INVISIBLE, not merely closed, because a seam splits and the syrup ends up in the pot. Bare simmer, never a rolling boil. And floating is only the first signal — give them 2 to 3 minutes more, then into the coconut while still dripping wet.","pairsWith":"Hot unsweetened tea, and specifically something plain and slightly tannic rather than anything creamy or spiced — these are intensely sweet and the tea is there to cut them, which is exactly how they are sold at a market with a glass of black tea. They belong on a plate with other small market sweets rather than alone, and a few alongside sliced fresh fruit makes a very good end to a rich meal, because there is no dairy and no pastry in them at all. Strong black coffee works for the same reason as the tea.","trivia":"The green is the interesting part, and it is not food colouring. Klepon is coloured and scented with pandan, the long strap-like leaf of Pandanus amaryllifolius, which is one of very few leaves anywhere in the world used the way vanilla is used — as a sweet aromatic in its own right rather than as a herb. What makes it remarkable is the specific compound responsible: 2-acetyl-1-pyrroline. That is the same molecule that gives jasmine rice and basmati their smell, and the same molecule that gives the crust of a freshly baked loaf its smell. It is why pandan is so hard to describe to anyone who has not met it and so instantly familiar once they have — the brain has filed it under new bread and hot rice. The leaves are sold frozen in Asian grocers, and one knotted leaf dropped into the cooking water, or a few blitzed with the dough water and strained, gives both the colour and the scent. Without it you have a perfectly good white klepon, which is also traditional and is what most home cooks make.\n\nKlepon belongs to jajan pasar, the Javanese market-snack tradition — dozens of small steamed, boiled and fried sweets sold from trays before dawn and eaten by the handful, of which klepon is among the oldest and simplest. The naming is a proper tangle worth knowing if you ever shop for them. In Java it is klepon. Across Sumatra, in Malaysia and in Singapore the same thing is called onde-onde, and in parts of Malaysia buah melaka. But in Java, onde-onde means something else entirely — a fried sesame-covered ball with a sweet bean filling, which is a Chinese-descended snack and a completely different food. So the same word names two unrelated sweets depending on which island you are standing on, and both are sold in the same country.","howThisFeels":"The half-second of ordinary chewiness before it goes, and then hot dark syrup arriving all at once with nowhere to put it.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold klepon come most of the way back with 30 seconds of steam — enough to soften the dough and re-melt the centre — which is far better than a microwave, where they go tough and the sugar boils out.","(easy) Split cold ones in half and press them cut-side down into a hot dry pan for a minute until the flat face crisps and caramelises, which turns a stale sweet into a better one.","(medium) Dropped into a bowl of warm coconut milk sweetened with a little jaggery and a pinch of salt, which is essentially kolak and makes leftovers into a pudding.","(creative) Leftover shaped raw balls are the real prize rather than leftover cooked ones — boil four straight from the freezer and you have a hot dessert in seven minutes with no preparation at all."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Klepon Putih — Plain White, No Pandan (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":10,"howThisFeels":"Plain white and slightly startling, because nothing about the look prepares you for what is inside.","trivia":"The white version is not the compromise it looks like — it is what most Javanese households actually make, because pandan is a garden plant you cut when you have it and skip when you do not. Leaving it out changes the dish less than you would expect, since pandan is an aroma rather than a flavour with any weight to it, and what you notice is the colour more than the taste. It also makes a good argument for the salt in the coconut: with no pandan there is nothing at all standing between you and pure sugar except that salt, so it stops being a background detail and becomes the thing keeping the sweet in check.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"30g jaggery","to":"25g jaggery"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No pandan at all, and reduce the jaggery slightly to 25g rather than 30g. The reason is worth understanding rather than just following: pandan's aroma does a surprising amount of work distracting from pure sweetness, and without it the same quantity of sugar reads as noticeably sweeter. Everything else is identical — the hard dry nuggets, the invisible seal, the bare simmer, the two minutes after floating, the coconut while wet. If you want a little of the aroma without buying anything, drop a strip of lime zest into the boiling water; it is not pandan and does not pretend to be, but it gives the dough a faint fragrance and keeps the dish from tasting one-note."}]}},{"name":"Klepon — Pandan and Palm Sugar","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":11,"howThisFeels":"Vivid green, still warm, and the coconut coming off on your fingers before you have even picked one up.","trivia":"There is a real technical reason klepon is boiled rather than steamed, even though most of its market-tray siblings are steamed. Boiling gives the ball a hard outer skin of gelatinised starch within the first minute, sealing the seam mechanically while the inside is still raw — the water is doing part of the job your palms did. It also gives you the single most useful doneness cue in the whole jajan pasar repertoire, the float, which a steamer cannot offer at all. And the melted sugar needs the sustained 100°C of surrounding water to liquefy fully in the few minutes available; a steamer's gentler heat leaves the nugget half-dissolved and grainy."},{"name":"Klepon Ubi — Sweet Potato Dough (Vegan)","icon":"🍠","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":12,"howThisFeels":"Softer, orange and a little fudgy, and it gives way sooner so the syrup arrives faster.","trivia":"Adding sweet potato is a standard trick across the region and it is not just about colour or thrift. The cooked potato brings moisture and its own sugars, so the dough is softer, more pliable and much more forgiving to seal — this is the version to make if a batch has split on you before. It also changes the eating: a straight glutinous-flour ball is properly chewy and takes a moment to break, while a sweet potato dough yields almost immediately, which means the syrup releases faster and the whole thing feels less like a trick and more like a pudding. The trade is that it stales faster, because the extra moisture accelerates the dough going firm, so these want eating within a couple of hours rather than six.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"90g glutinous rice flour","to":"60g glutinous rice flour"},{"from":"70ml water","to":"20ml water"}],"addIng":[{"item":"80g sweet potato"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cook the sweet potato first and account for its water. Steam or boil 80g of sweet potato, peeled and cut into chunks, until completely soft — 12 to 15 minutes — then drain it thoroughly and let it steam dry for a couple of minutes before mashing it smooth while hot. Wet potato means wet dough, so do not rush that drying. Mix the hot mash into the 60g of glutinous rice flour with the salt, then add the 20ml of water only as needed, a teaspoon at a time; depending on the potato you may not need all of it, and this is the one version where the written water figure is genuinely a maximum rather than a target. Knead to the same soft modelling-clay feel. Everything else is unchanged — hard sugar nuggets, invisible seam, bare simmer, two to three minutes past the float, coconut while wet. These float slightly sooner because the dough is less dense, so watch the clock from the float rather than from the drop."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Perkedel","target":"indonesia-perkedel","emoji":"🥔"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-bakso","name":"Bakso","nameAlt":"Bakso — the bounce is not a trick of the recipe but a protein gel, and you build it with cold, salt and mechanical violence","aliases":["Bakso","Baso","Indonesian Meatballs","Bakso Sapi","Bakso Mie","Bouncy Beef Meatballs","Bakso Kuah"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","street-food","beef","noodles","comfort","make-ahead"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","lunch","guests","winter"],"ingredients":"200g beef mince · 500ml stock · 80g noodles · 30ml water · 20g tapioca starch · 10g garlic · 10g spring onions · 5g salt · 3g white pepper · 1 large egg","method":"A proper bakso does something no other meatball does: you press it and it pushes back, and when you bite it there is a distinct springy snap rather than a crumble. That texture has a name in Indonesia, kenyal, and it is the single thing vendors are judged on. It is also not a matter of skill or of a secret ingredient. It is a protein gel, it obeys rules, and the rules are all about temperature. Read this paragraph before you start, because once the meat is out of the fridge the clock is running.\n\nHere is what you are actually making. Muscle contains a protein called myosin, locked inside the fibres. Salt dissolves it out. Once free, and once worked mechanically, those long protein molecules unravel and link into a continuous three-dimensional net, and when that net is heated it sets — trapping water and fat inside an elastic mesh. That mesh is the bounce. Every instruction below either helps that net form or stops it collapsing. And the thing that destroys it is heat: above roughly 12°C the fat in the mince softens and smears, coating the protein and physically blocking it from linking up. Smeared fat gives you a meatball that is grainy, crumbly and slightly greasy — the classic failure, and there is no fixing it once it has happened.\n\nSo begin by getting everything cold, colder than feels reasonable. Put the 200g of beef mince in the freezer for 20 to 30 minutes until it is firm at the edges and just beginning to crystallise but not frozen solid. Put the bowl and the blade of your food processor in the freezer too, and put the 30ml of water in the freezer or add an ice cube to it — you want it barely above freezing. If you have no processor, this can be done in a mortar or by chopping and slapping the meat on a board with a heavy knife or cleaver, which is the traditional method and works well, but it is slower, so keep the meat in the fridge and work in two batches.\n\nUse beef with some fat in it, around 15 to 20 percent — ordinary lean mince is fine, extra-lean is not. Fat is what makes the set gel taste of something and stay tender rather than rubbery; the point of keeping it cold is not to remove it but to keep it in solid pieces until the protein net has already formed around it.\n\nNow the order of adding things, which is not negotiable. Put the near-frozen mince, the 10g of garlic, the 5g of salt and the 3g of white pepper into the cold processor bowl and blitz. THE SALT GOES IN AT THE START, WITH THE MEAT. This is the step most recipes get wrong by treating salt as seasoning to be added to taste at the end. Salt is the reagent here — nothing dissolves the myosin out of the fibres except salt, and if it arrives after the mixing is done there is no time for it to work and no bounce at all. Five grams to 200g of meat is a genuine 2.5 percent, which is high for a meatball and is exactly the level a sausage-maker uses, for exactly this reason.\n\nBlitz in short bursts for about a minute until the meat has gone from crumbly and separate to a smooth, dense, sticky paste. Then add the 20g of tapioca starch and the egg, and with the machine running pour in the ice-cold 30ml of water in a thin stream. Keep going another minute. Stop and feel the bowl with the back of your hand at any point — if it feels anything other than cold, stop, put the whole thing back in the freezer for ten minutes, and carry on afterwards. Interrupting is free. Overheating is terminal.\n\nTapioca starch rather than flour or cornflour is deliberate and it is the second half of the texture. It gelatinises into a clear, springy, slightly slippery gel rather than the soft opaque one wheat flour gives, so it reinforces the protein net instead of padding it. Twenty grams to 200g of meat is about the upper limit of honest — more starch makes a bouncier ball more cheaply, which is precisely why cheap commercial bakso is springy and tastes of almost nothing, and past about 15 percent you are eating starch with beef flavouring.\n\nThe paste is ready when it is visibly tacky and pulls back at you: push a spoon through it and the surface should close behind the spoon rather than staying open. There is a test if you want certainty — drop a marble-sized piece into a bowl of cold water. It should hold a tight round shape and sit firm rather than spreading or falling apart. If it spreads, the mixture is too warm or under-worked; chill it and work it again.\n\nBring a wide pan of water to a BARE SIMMER — steam and the occasional lazy bubble, around 85 to 90°C, and never a boil. This matters as much as the cold did. A rolling boil makes the setting protein net contract hard and squeeze out the water it was supposed to be holding, and the ball comes out tough, dry and pitted with little tunnels. Gentle heat sets the gel without wringing it.\n\nShape and cook in one motion, which is how the carts do it and it is genuinely easier than shaping first. Wet one hand, take a handful of paste, and squeeze it up through the gap between your thumb and forefinger so a smooth ball forms at the top; scoop that ball off with a wet spoon and lower it straight into the water. Around 3cm across is right. Work quickly and keep the bowl of paste in the fridge or over ice between handfuls.\n\nThey will sink. After 6 to 8 minutes they will rise and float, and unlike a dumpling, FLOATING HERE MEANS DONE — the ball becomes buoyant because the gel has set and trapped its water. Give them one more minute at most and lift them out. Overcooking is the last way to lose the texture: past about twelve minutes the net keeps tightening and the springiness turns to rubber.\n\nNow build the bowl, and keep the poaching water. Bring the 500ml of stock to a simmer, and add a ladle or two of the cloudy poaching liquid to it — that liquid has dissolved meat protein in it and it is the difference between a bakso broth and hot stock from a carton. Season it and keep it clear; bakso broth is meant to be thin and light, not enriched, because the meatballs are the substantial part. Cook the 80g of noodles separately, in their own water, and drain them, because starch released into the broth makes it cloudy and gluey.\n\nAssemble in the bowl rather than in the pot: noodles first, then the meatballs, then the hot broth poured over, then the 10g of sliced spring onions on top. Serve with sambal, and with a small spoonful of the broth first before you add anything to it, because you should be able to taste it plain.","cookTime":"Freeze meat 30 min · Blitz 5 min · Poach 8 min · Assemble 10 min","kcal":"~560 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 44g, Carbs 44g, Fat 21g, Sodium ~1420mg","storage":"The meatballs keep far better than most things made of minced beef, and the reason is the gel — a set protein net holds its water rather than weeping, so poached bakso stay firm and springy for 4 days in the fridge in a closed container, ideally with a little of their poaching liquid. They freeze extremely well for 3 months and lose almost nothing; freeze them apart on a tray first, then bag them, and reheat straight from frozen in simmering broth for 4 to 5 minutes. Never reheat them in a rolling boil or a microwave: the first tightens the gel into rubber and the second does it faster. The raw paste is a different matter and must be used the day it is made — it will not wait, and freezing it raw is worse than pointless because the ice crystals rupture the protein net you just built and the bounce is simply gone. Store the components apart: noodles sitting in broth turn to porridge within an hour, so cooked noodles go in their own container for up to 3 days and the broth keeps 4 days or freezes 3 months. Assembled bowls do not keep at all.","chefNotes":"Cold is the whole recipe. Near-freeze the mince, chill the bowl and blade, use ice water, and stop the moment anything feels warm — above about 12°C the fat smears and the bounce is gone for good. Salt goes in AT THE START with the meat, because salt is what dissolves the myosin out; salt added at the end seasons but does nothing. Tapioca starch, not flour, and no more than 20g to 200g of meat. Then poach at a bare 85–90°C, never a boil, and lift them out a minute after they float.","pairsWith":"Sambal is not optional at a bakso cart — a spoonful stirred into the broth is how nearly everyone eats it, along with a splash of sweet soy and a squeeze of lime, all added at the table so each bowl ends up different. Fried shallots scattered on top add the crunch the bowl otherwise lacks. Perkedel on the side, broken into the broth, is a standard and very good addition. Beyond that it wants nothing: it is a complete bowl and adding a side dish is missing the point of a one-bowl meal eaten standing next to a cart.","trivia":"Three cuisines, working separately and centuries apart, arrived at the same piece of food science, and bakso is one of them. The springiness of a bakso, the snap of a good frankfurter, and the particular firm bounce of Japanese fish cake are all the same phenomenon: cold protein plus salt plus mechanical work, producing an elastic gel that sets on heating. A German sausage-maker chilling the bowl, an Indonesian cart cook slapping beef with a cleaver on a wet board, and a Japanese cook pounding fish paste are all doing one thing, and none of them needed to know the word myosin to work out that the mixture must be kept cold and salted early. It is one of the clearest cases in cooking of technique arriving well ahead of the explanation.\n\nWhat makes Indonesia unusual is that the country turned that texture into the entire basis of judgement. Bakso is not evaluated on seasoning or on broth in the way a ramen or a pho would be; it is evaluated on kenyal, the bounce, and vendors build reputations on it. Push a bakso against the side of the bowl with a spoon and watch whether it springs back is a completely normal thing to do before eating. The consequence is an arms race, and not always an honourable one: since starch produces bounce far more cheaply than beef does, the springiest bakso on a street is quite often the one with the least meat in it, which is exactly why 20g of tapioca to 200g of beef is the honest ceiling rather than a starting point.\n\nThe name gives away where it came from — bakso is Hokkien, from bak-so, meaning minced meat, brought by Chinese traders and settlers. Its ancestor is a pork meatball, and the single change that made it Indonesian was the protein: beef, so that it could be sold to a Muslim-majority country from a cart on a public street. That one substitution is why bakso is beef almost everywhere in Indonesia while the dish it descends from is pork. The carts themselves became an institution, wheeled gerobak announcing themselves down residential streets by rapping a spoon against a bowl, and the form keeps mutating — the current street spectacle is bakso beranak, a single enormous meatball with smaller meatballs sealed inside it, cut open at the table.","howThisFeels":"Pressing one against the side of the bowl with the back of a spoon just to watch it spring back, which is a thing you will do without being told after the first one.","leftovers":["(easy) Meatballs dropped straight from the fridge into any hot clear broth with a handful of greens — four minutes and it is dinner again, and they are arguably better on day two because the gel firms further.","(easy) Halved and fried cut-side down in a dry hot pan until the flat faces brown and caramelise, then eaten with sambal as a snack, which is a completely different and very good use for them.","(medium) Sliced thin and stir-fried with cabbage, garlic and kecap manis into a fast noodle dish, where the springy slices behave more like a sausage than a meatball.","(creative) Skewered three to a stick, brushed with kecap manis and grilled hard over coals until blistered — bakso bakar, a real Indonesian street form and the best possible thing to do with the last six in the container."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Bakso Kuah — Meatballs and Broth Only (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42,"howThisFeels":"Clear, hot and startlingly plain, with nothing in the bowl to distract from the texture you just built.","trivia":"Without noodles this becomes the older and simpler form, and it is the one to make the first time because there is nothing hiding the meatball. It is also how a cart serves it to someone who wants a snack rather than a meal — five or six balls in a paper bowl with broth and a spoonful of sambal. There is a practical advantage too: no noodles means no starch anywhere near the broth, so it stays properly clear and light, which is how bakso broth is supposed to be. If you want to make it more of a meal without the noodles, a handful of shredded cabbage or greens wilted in the broth for a minute at the end is the traditional move.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"80g noodles"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Leave the noodles out entirely and the bowl gets simpler rather than emptier. Everything about the cold meat, the early salt, the tapioca and the bare-simmer poach is unchanged. Because there is no starch going into the bowl, be slightly more generous with the poaching liquid stirred into the stock — three or four ladles rather than one or two — since that dissolved meat protein is now carrying the whole broth. Wilt a handful of shredded cabbage or any leafy green in the simmering broth for 60 seconds before serving and lift it into the bowl first, which gives the bowl some body without clouding it. Serve with more sambal than you think you need."}]}},{"name":"Bakso Mie — with Noodles","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":49,"howThisFeels":"A whole bowl arriving at once — hot thin broth, springy balls and noodles that have not been allowed to go anywhere near each other until this second.","trivia":"Cooking the noodles in their own separate water and never in the broth is the one rule that separates a good bowl from a mediocre one, and it is worth understanding rather than obeying. Noodles shed a surprising quantity of surface starch, and starch in a thin broth does two things: it clouds it, and it thickens it slightly so it coats the mouth and dulls everything else. Bakso broth is deliberately light because the meatballs are the substance — a thickened broth flattens the contrast the whole dish is built on. It is the same reason the assembly happens in the bowl rather than in the pot: anything that sits together for ten minutes tastes like one thing instead of three."},{"name":"Bakso Jamur — Mushroom and Tofu (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":54,"howThisFeels":"Dense, deeply savoury and satisfying in its own right — softer than the beef, and honest about being a different thing.","trivia":"This version has to be honest about one thing: it cannot bounce, and any recipe claiming otherwise is selling you something. The spring in a bakso comes from myosin, an animal muscle protein, and there is no myosin in a mushroom or a soya bean — no amount of cold or salt or pounding will produce that net. So the goal changes rather than being chased: instead of an elastic gel you are building a dense, well-bound, deeply savoury ball, and the binding is done by starch and by pressing rather than by protein. Mushrooms are the right partner because they bring glutamates, the same savoury compounds that make the beef version taste meaty, and squeezing the water out of them is what stops the mixture being a paste that falls apart.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"200g beef mince"},{"item":"1 large egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"150g mushrooms"},{"item":"120g firm tofu"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"20g tapioca starch","to":"35g tapioca starch"}],"addStep":[{"text":"The cold no longer matters and the squeezing does, so the whole approach changes. Chop the 150g of mushrooms finely and fry them in a dry pan over high heat for 6 to 8 minutes until they have released their water and it has evaporated and they begin to brown and squeak — this is the single most important step, because mushrooms are around 90 percent water and unsqueezed mushroom makes a mixture that will not hold a shape at any starch level. Let them cool. Press the 120g of firm tofu hard between two plates for 15 minutes, pour off the water, then crumble it and squeeze it in a clean cloth until no more comes out. Blitz the cooled mushrooms, the squeezed tofu, the garlic, the salt and the white pepper to a coarse thick paste — coarse, not smooth, because the texture here comes from the pieces rather than from a gel. Work in 35g of tapioca starch rather than 20g; with no protein net the starch is doing all the binding, and it needs the extra. Skip the water entirely unless the mixture will not come together. Roll firmly into balls by hand, pressing hard so they are dense, and poach them in the same bare 85–90°C water for 8 to 10 minutes. They will not float reliably, so time them instead. Use a well-seasoned vegetable stock and be generous with the sambal, the sweet soy and the fried shallots at the table, all of which are doing more work in this bowl than in the beef one."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Perkedel","target":"indonesia-perkedel","emoji":"🥔"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-rawon","name":"Rawon","nameAlt":"Rawon — a jet-black beef soup coloured entirely by a nut that is lethal until it has been buried for a month, and the first job is rejecting the bad ones","aliases":["Rawon","Rawon Surabaya","Nasi Rawon","Black Beef Soup","Indonesian Black Soup","Rawon Rusuk","Rawon Setan"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","beef","slow-cooked","spiced","make-ahead","festive"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","sunday","guests","celebration","winter"],"ingredients":"180g beef brisket · 500ml water · 40g bean sprouts · 20g shallots · 20ml sunflower oil · 15g red chilli · 12g keluak · 10g garlic · 10g lemongrass · 10ml lime juice · 8g galangal · 5g ground turmeric · 5g tamarind paste · 4g salt · 2 bay leaves","method":"The black is not burnt, not soy, not squid ink and not colouring. It is one ingredient — keluak, the seed of Pangium edule — and it is the only thing in this recipe that cannot be substituted or approximated. Everything else here is an ordinary beef soup. So the first job is the nuts, and it is a job of rejection rather than preparation.\n\nBUY THEM ALREADY BLACK AND ALREADY PROCESSED, which is the only way they are sold. Raw keluak is genuinely poisonous and the detoxification is not a home process — it takes boiling followed by weeks buried in ash or earth, and the black colour is the evidence that it has happened. Nothing in this recipe involves treating a raw nut, and if you are ever offered fresh cream-coloured ones, they are not food.\n\nNow inspect them one at a time, and this is the actual technique. Crack each shell — the back of a heavy knife or a hammer, on a board, they are hard — and scoop out the flesh. GOOD FLESH IS SOFT, ALMOST PASTE-LIKE, DEEP BLACK OR VERY DARK CHOCOLATE BROWN, AND SMELLS FAINTLY OF TAMARIND, COCOA AND SOY. Reject any nut whose flesh is pale, cream, grey, hard, chalky or dry, and reject any that smells sharp, bitter or faintly of almonds. Those are the under-processed ones and they are both the flavour risk and the safety risk. Throw them out and do not try to rescue them by cooking them longer. A single bad nut will make an entire pot taste acrid and there is no fixing it afterwards, which is why the nuts are checked individually and why you should buy a few more than you need. You want 12g of good flesh, which is usually three or four nuts.\n\nMash that flesh with about 40ml of the water into a smooth, loose black paste, then PUSH IT THROUGH A SIEVE and discard what stays behind. The flesh carries fibres and small fragments of shell, and both are gritty in a finished soup in a way that reads as carelessness. This takes a minute and it is the difference between a silky black broth and one that feels sandy on the teeth.\n\nNow the beef, and here is the structural decision that most versions get wrong: RAWON IS A SOUP, NOT A CURRY. The mistake is to fry everything together and stew it into a thick dark gravy, which produces something perfectly edible that is not this dish. Rawon should be a thin, clean, intensely black broth that you could drink from a cup, with tender beef sitting in it. That means building a clear stock first and colouring it second.\n\nCut the 180g of beef brisket into 3cm cubes. Brisket, or another well-worked cut with fat and collagen in it — this simmers for a long time and a lean cut will simply go dry and fibrous. Put it in a pot with the remaining water, bring it to the boil, and then SKIM. For the first five minutes a grey foam will rise; lift it off and discard it, repeatedly, until the surface runs clear. That foam is coagulated protein and impurities, and if it is stirred back in it clouds the broth permanently — which you will not be able to see under the black, but you will taste as a slight muddiness. Once it is clear, drop the heat to a bare simmer.\n\nWhile that goes, make the bumbu. Pound or blitz the 20g of shallots, the 10g of garlic, the 15g of red chilli, the 8g of galangal, the trimmed pale third of the 10g of lemongrass sliced thinly, the 5g of ground turmeric and the 4g of salt into a rough paste. Fry it in the 20ml of sunflower oil over medium heat for 4 to 5 minutes, stirring, until it smells rounded rather than raw and stops looking wet. Then add the sieved keluak paste to the pan and fry it for another 2 minutes. Frying the keluak rather than tipping it straight into the pot matters: it is a starchy, faintly bitter paste raw, and a couple of minutes in hot oil turns the bitterness into something closer to cocoa and unlocks the colour, which deepens visibly in the pan from brown-black to true black.\n\nScrape all of it into the simmering beef, add the 5g of tamarind paste and the 2 bay leaves, and simmer uncovered, gently, for 90 minutes to 2 hours until the brisket is completely tender and gives to a spoon. Uncovered, because a little reduction concentrates the broth, and gently, because a hard boil emulsifies the fat into the liquid and makes it cloudy and greasy instead of clean. Top up with hot water if it drops below the beef. Taste for salt at the end, not the beginning.\n\nThe bay leaves are standing in for daun salam and they are a fair stand-in, but use two and not four — the same quantity that suits the milder Indonesian leaf will push a eucalyptus note through a broth this delicate.\n\nStir in the 10ml of lime juice off the heat, right at the end. Acid added during the simmer dulls the black towards grey-brown; added at the end it lifts a soup that is otherwise deep, dark and slightly heavy, and it is the difference between rawon that tastes finished and rawon that tastes like it needs something.\n\nServe it with the 40g of bean sprouts RAW, put into the bowl before the hot broth goes over them, or handed round separately. This is not a garnish and it is worth insisting on. The whole dish is soft, dark and savoury, and short raw bean sprouts are the only crunch and the only fresh note in it — they wilt slightly in the heat and keep their snap. Cooked into the pot they collapse and contribute nothing. Traditionally these are the small stubby sprouts rather than the long ones, and if yours are long, snap them in half.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Skim 5 min · Simmer 2 hr","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g, Carbs 15g, Fat 33g, Sodium ~1180mg","storage":"Rawon is genuinely better on day two, and unusually for a soup the reason is the keluak — its flavour keeps moving into the beef and the broth overnight, and the slight bitterness it carries on the first day softens into something rounder. Cool it uncovered to room temperature and refrigerate in a closed container for up to 4 days. It will set with a dark cap of fat on top; stir that back in on reheating, it is where a good deal of the flavour lives. Reheat gently to a bare simmer, never a rolling boil, for the same reason as the first cook — hard heat emulsifies the fat and turns a clean broth greasy. It freezes very well for 3 months with no loss at all, which makes a double batch the sensible way to cook it given how long it takes and how dear the nuts are. ⛔ Store the bean sprouts entirely separately and add them raw to each bowl — sprouts left sitting in hot broth go limp within ten minutes and slimy by the next day, and they are the only texture the dish has. Cooked rice also keeps separately, never in the soup.","chefNotes":"The nuts are the whole dish, so inspect them one by one and throw out any that are pale, hard, chalky or sharp-smelling — one bad nut ruins the pot and there is no rescuing it. Sieve the mashed keluak or the broth will be gritty. Then remember it is a SOUP, not a curry: skim the beef until the surface runs clear, fry the bumbu and the keluak separately, and only then bring them together. Lime juice off the heat at the very end. Bean sprouts raw in the bowl, never cooked in the pot.","pairsWith":"Plain steamed white rice, either alongside or with the soup ladled straight over it, which is how it is usually eaten and is why the dish is often called nasi rawon. The traditional plate carries a salted duck egg cut in half, a spoonful of sambal terasi, and prawn crackers, and every one of those is there to push against the darkness of the broth — salt, heat and crunch respectively. A wedge of lime on the side lets each person sharpen their own bowl. Fried tempe alongside works for the same reason the sprouts do.","trivia":"The same tree that gives this dish its colour was used across the archipelago to poison fish. Pangium edule seeds are loaded with cyanogenic glycosides, compounds that release hydrogen cyanide when the seed tissue is crushed, and crushed seeds thrown into a pool would stun or kill everything in it. Raw keluak is not a mild risk; it is dangerous. What makes rawon extraordinary is that somebody worked out how to disarm it — boil the nuts, then bury them in ash or damp earth and leave them for a month or more, during which the tissue breaks down, the toxins degrade and disperse, and the cream-coloured flesh turns black. The colour is the receipt. It is one of the most striking pieces of food technology anywhere: not a preservation method, not a flavour method, but a deliberate multi-week detoxification, arrived at without any understanding of what was being removed, and then built into a national dish rather than merely tolerated. The nuts are sold ready-processed everywhere they are sold at all, which is why no recipe asks you to do any of this.\n\nRawon itself belongs to East Java and to Surabaya in particular, and it is old — a dish under that name appears in the Javanese Nagarakretagama-era records and in inscriptions discussed as far back as the tenth century, which makes it one of the oldest continuously eaten Indonesian dishes with a recognisable identity. The accompaniments are unusually fixed for Indonesian food, where almost everything else is negotiable at the cart: short raw bean sprouts, a salted duck egg, sambal and crackers appear together so consistently that a bowl without them looks wrong. There is also a small piece of Surabaya restaurant folklore worth knowing — rawon setan, devil's rawon, named not for its heat but for the hours, since the original stall was known for serving it in the middle of the night. And the colour has a practical consequence nobody mentions: keluak stains. It will mark a wooden spoon, a plastic chopping board and a white shirt permanently, which is a reasonable price for the only naturally jet-black soup in the world.","howThisFeels":"Lifting the lid two hours in and finding something genuinely black in the pot, then the crack of raw bean sprouts against beef that falls apart on the spoon.","leftovers":["(easy) Better the next day as it is — reheat gently to a bare simmer and add fresh raw sprouts, and it will taste rounder and less bitter than it did on the first evening.","(easy) Beef lifted out, shredded, and piled onto hot rice with a spoonful of the reduced broth over it, which turns a soup into a rice bowl.","(medium) Broth reduced hard by half in an open pan until it is thick and glossy, then used as a black sauce over noodles or fried tempe — an entirely different dish out of what is left.","(creative) Shredded beef fried in a little oil with kecap manis and chilli until the edges go crisp, then folded through fried rice, where the black bumbu still clinging to the meat colours everything faintly grey-purple and tastes remarkable."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Rawon Bumbu Instan — the Sachet Route (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":54,"howThisFeels":"Black in the pot in twenty minutes of work instead of an hour, and honestly close.","trivia":"This is the one shortcut in Indonesian cooking that is genuinely worth taking, and the reason is that a bumbu rawon sachet actually contains keluak — it is not a seasoning standing in for the nut but a different way of buying the same thing. That makes the arithmetic very different from most convenience products: a sachet costs about a tenth of what a pack of whole nuts costs to buy, and works out cheaper per plate as well. What you give up is control and the black is usually a shade less intense, because the keluak content is fixed and modest and there is no frying step to deepen it. What you do not give up is the dish. The sachets are specialty-importer stock rather than supermarket shelf, so it is worth buying two when you find them.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"12g keluak"},{"item":"8g galangal"},{"item":"10g lemongrass"},{"item":"5g ground turmeric"}],"addIng":[{"item":"12g rawon spice mix"}],"addStep":[{"text":"The nut inspection, the sieving and the separate bumbu all disappear. Skim the beef exactly as described — that step is about the broth and no sachet does it for you, and it is half of what makes rawon a soup rather than a stew. Fry the 20g of shallots, the 10g of garlic and the 15g of red chilli in the oil for 3 minutes, then add the 12g of rawon spice mix and fry it for a further 90 seconds, stirring; even a paste from a packet is better for a short fry, because it takes the flat raw edge off and deepens the colour a little. Scrape it into the skimmed beef with the tamarind and bay leaves and simmer the same 90 minutes to 2 hours. Expect a slightly softer, less inky black and a rounder, less bitter flavour. Push the lime juice up to 15ml at the end — the sachet versions are usually a touch sweeter and flatter, and the extra acid is what brings them back into line. Raw bean sprouts in the bowl, unchanged and non-negotiable."}]}},{"name":"Rawon Surabaya — Whole Keluak","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":84,"howThisFeels":"Cracking nuts open one by one and throwing two away, which feels wasteful right up until you taste what the good ones do.","trivia":"Frying the sieved keluak paste in oil before it goes anywhere near the pot is the step that separates a properly black rawon from a murky brown one, and it is worth knowing why rather than taking it on trust. Raw keluak paste is starchy and carries a distinct bitterness; two minutes in hot fat does two things at once — it cooks that starch out, which is what lets the colour disperse cleanly through a broth instead of clouding it, and it converts the raw bitterness into something much closer to dark chocolate. You can watch it happen: the paste shifts from a dull brown-black to a glossy true black in the pan. Tipped in raw, the same nuts give a soup that is greyer, grittier and faintly harsh, and no amount of extra simmering corrects it."},{"name":"Rawon Rusuk — Short Rib on the Bone","icon":"🦴","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":89,"howThisFeels":"A bone standing up out of a black bowl, and broth with enough body to coat the back of a spoon.","trivia":"Rusuk means rib, and this is the version restaurants in Surabaya charge more for. The difference is not the meat but the bone. Beef ribs carry marrow and a great deal of collagen in the connective tissue around them, and over a long gentle simmer both dissolve into the broth — the collagen as gelatine, which gives the liquid a faint silkiness and body that a cube of brisket cannot, and the marrow as fat and flavour. It is the same reason a bone broth behaves differently from a meat stock. The trade is time and patience: bone-in meat needs a longer simmer to reach the same tenderness, and the skimming matters more at the start because bones throw far more foam than muscle does.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g beef brisket","to":"220g beef short rib"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 220g of beef short rib rather than 180g of brisket — the extra weight is bone, so the edible meat is roughly the same. Cut between the bones into individual pieces if they come as a rack. The skimming at the start becomes the important part rather than a tidy habit: bones release considerably more grey foam than muscle, and it will keep rising for a good eight or ten minutes rather than five, so stay with it and keep lifting it off until the surface genuinely runs clear. Then simmer 2 hours 30 minutes to 3 hours, gently and uncovered, until the meat is pulling back from the bone and a skewer slides through it with no resistance. Do not rush it with a harder boil; the collagen needs time at low heat to become gelatine, and a hard boil just makes the broth cloudy. Skim the fat off the top before serving if you want it cleaner, or leave it, which is what most places do. Everything else is identical, including the lime off the heat and the raw sprouts in the bowl."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"}]}
];
